<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix an integer <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. In this paper, we study the topology of a space <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> that parametrizes isomorphism classes of genus <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tropical curves</span> of volume 1. Tropical curves are certain weighted, marked metric graphs; see §<a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1. Weighted graphs and tropical curves ‣ 2. Graphs, tropical curves, and moduli ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> for the precise definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Interest in the space <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is not limited to tropical geometry. Indeed, <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> may be identified homeomorphically with the following spaces:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the link of the vertex in the tropical moduli space <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACP15</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">BMV11</a>]</cite>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the dual complex of the boundary divisor in <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, the algebraic moduli space of stable curves of genus <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> (Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem7" title="Corollary 5.7. ‣ 5.2. Dual complexes of normal crossings divisors ‣ 5. Boundary complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>);</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the quotient of the simplicial completion of Culler–Vogtmann outer space by the action of the outer automorphism group <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Out(F_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Out</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Out(F_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">CV03</a>, §5.2]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vog15</a>, §2.2]</cite>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the topological quotient of Harvey’s complex of curves on a surface of genus <math id="S1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> by the action of the mapping class group <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har81</a>]</cite>; and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the topological quotient of Hatcher’s complex of sphere systems in certain a 3-manifold by the action of the mapping class group of that manifold <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hat95</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our primary focus will be on the interpretations (1) and especially (2) from tropical and algebraic geometry: we apply combinatorial topological calculations on <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to compute previously unknown invariants of the complex algebraic moduli space <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. One such application gives a lower bound on the size of <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The cohomology <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=5</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 7</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> grows at least exponentially. More precisely,</span></p>
<table id="S1.SS0.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.SS0.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})&gt;\beta^{g}+\text{constant}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">constant</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})&gt;\beta^{g}+\text{constant}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for any <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&lt;\beta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&lt;\beta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0}\approx 1.3247\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>1.3247</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0}\approx 1.3247\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> is the real root of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{3}-t-1=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{3}-t-1=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The nonvanishing for <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math> was known previously; Looijenga famously showed that the unstable part of <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{6}(\mathcal{M}_{3};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{6}(\mathcal{M}_{3};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> has rank 1 and weight 12 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loo93</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To put Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> in context, recall that the virtual cohomological dimension of <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4g-5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4g-5</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har86</a>]</cite>. Church, Farb, and Putman conjectured that, for each fixed <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-4-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-4-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes for all but finitely many <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">CFP14</a>, Conjecture 9]</cite>. While this is true for <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">CFP12</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">MSS13</a>]</cite>, Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> shows that it is false for <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, as observed by Morita, Sakasai, and Suzuki <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">MSS15</a>, Remark 7.5]</cite>, the Church-Farb-Putman conjecture is implied by a more general statement conjectured by Kontsevich two decades earlier <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kon93</a>, Conjecture 7C]</cite>, which we now recall. In the same paper where he introduced the graph complex, Kontsevich studied three infinite dimensional Lie algebras, whose homologies are free
graded commutative algebras generated by subspaces of primitive elements. Each contains the primitive homology of the Lie algebra <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sp}(2\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sp}(2\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> as a direct summand.
For one of these Lie algebras, denoted <math id="S1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, the complementary primitive homology is</p>
<table id="S1.SS0.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.SS0.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PH_{k}(a_{\infty})/PH_{k}(\mathrm{sp}(2\infty))\ \cong\bigoplus_{m&gt;0,2g-2+m&gt;0}H^{4g-4+2m-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,m}/S_{m};\mathbb{Q})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>sp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PH_{k}(a_{\infty})/PH_{k}(\mathrm{sp}(2\infty))\ \cong\bigoplus_{m&gt;0,2g-2+m&gt;0}H^{4g-4+2m-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,m}/S_{m};\mathbb{Q}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the symmetric group acting on the moduli space <math id="S1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,m}</annotation></semantics></math> of curves with <math id="S1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> marked points by permuting the markings. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kon93</a>, Theorem 1.1(2)]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Kontsevich conjectured that the homology of each of these Lie algebras should be finite dimensional in each degree. In particular, for each <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, the cohomology group <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-2-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,1};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-2-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g,1};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> should vanish for all but finitely many <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that the composition</p>
<table id="S1.SS0.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.SS0.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})\rightarrow H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,1};\mathbb{Q})\rightarrow H^{*+2}(\mathcal{M}_{g,1};\mathbb{Q})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})\rightarrow H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g,1};\mathbb{Q})\rightarrow H^{*+2}(\mathcal{M}_{g,1};\mathbb{Q}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where the second map is cup product with the Euler class, is injective. This is because further composing with Gysin pushforward to <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> gives multiplication by <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2-2g&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2-2g&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore,
Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> shows that <math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PH_{2}(a_{\infty})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PH_{2}(a_{\infty})</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite dimensional, disproving Kontsevich’s conjecture and giving a negative answer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">MSS15</a>, Problem 7.4]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, and further applications discussed in Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Applications ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, will be established via combinatorial topological calculations on the space <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, which may be identified with the <span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">dual complex</span> of the Deligne-Mumford stable curve compactification <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Throughout, we work with varieties and Deligne-Mumford stacks over <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recall that Deligne has defined a natural weight filtration on the rational singular cohomology of any complex algebraic variety which gives, together with the Hodge filtration on singular cohomology with complex coefficients, a mixed Hodge structure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Del71</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Del74</a>]</cite>.
The graded pieces of the weight filtration on the cohomology of a <math id="S1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional variety are supported in degrees between <math id="S1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d</annotation></semantics></math>, and we refer to the <math id="S1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2d</annotation></semantics></math>-graded piece, denoted <math id="S1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{2d}^{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{2d}^{W}</annotation></semantics></math>, as the <span id="S1.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">top weight cohomology</span>. We will use the interpretation of <math id="S1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as the dual complex of the boundary divisor in the Deligne–Mumford compactification of <math id="S1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to give an identification of its reduced rational homology with the top weight-graded piece of the cohomology of <math id="S1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is an isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S1.SS0.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.SS0.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{6g-6}^{W}H^{6g-6-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{\cong}\widetilde{H}_{k-1}(\Delta_{g};\mathbb{Q})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≅</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{6g-6}^{W}H^{6g-6-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{\cong}\widetilde{H}_{k-1}(\Delta_{g};\mathbb{Q}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">identifying the top graded piece of the weight filtration on the cohomology of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> with the reduced rational homology of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Our proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> produces a specific isomorphism, which is in fact induced by a proper map of topological spaces</p>
<table id="S1.SS0.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1.0.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.SS0.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\lambda}}{{\longrightarrow}}M_{g}^{\mathrm{trop}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mi>λ</mi></mover><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\lambda}}{{\longrightarrow}}M_{g}^{\mathrm{trop}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p9.2" class="ltx_p">defined using the hyperbolic model for <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, see §<a href="#S7.SS1" title="7.1. A map of spaces ‣ 7. Hyperbolic surfaces ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>. Compactly supported cohomology is functorial with respect to proper maps, so (<a href="#S1.SS0.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.0.1</span></a>) induces maps <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{*}\colon H^{k}_{c}(M_{g}^{\mathrm{trop}};\mathbb{Q})\to H^{k}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{*}\colon H^{k}_{c}(M_{g}^{\mathrm{trop}};\mathbb{Q})\to H^{k}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. Using Poincaré duality for <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and that <math id="S1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is the link of the cone point in <math id="S1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}^{\mathrm{trop}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}^{\mathrm{trop}}</annotation></semantics></math>, this gives</p>
<table id="S1.SS0.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.SS0.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{6g-6-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})\cong(H^{k}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q}))^{\vee}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\lambda_{*}}}{{\longrightarrow}}(H^{k}_{c}(M_{g}^{\mathrm{trop}};\mathbb{Q}))^{\vee}\cong\widetilde{H}_{k-1}(\Delta_{g};\mathbb{Q})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup><mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{6g-6-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})\cong(H^{k}_{c}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q}))^{\vee}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\lambda_{*}}}{{\longrightarrow}}(H^{k}_{c}(M_{g}^{\mathrm{trop}};\mathbb{Q}))^{\vee}\cong\widetilde{H}_{k-1}(\Delta_{g};\mathbb{Q}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p9.3" class="ltx_p">We will see that this is a surjection, factoring over the isomorphism stated in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>. In this sense (<a href="#S1.SS0.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.0.1</span></a>) will be a space-level refinement of the map in rational cohomology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The space <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is glued out of standard simplices <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> in a way that resembles <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes, except simplices may be glued to themselves along permutations of their vertices. We call such objects <em id="S1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">symmetric <math id="S1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes</em>, briefly reviewed in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Symmetric 
        
          Δ
        
      -complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. In particular, its rational homology may be calculated by a cellular chain complex.
We will relate the cellular chain complex computing reduced rational homology of <math id="S1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to the <em id="S1.p10.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">commutative graph complex</em> <math id="S1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math>, introduced by Kontsevich <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kon93</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kon94</a>]</cite>. The precise definition of <math id="S1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> is recalled in Section <a href="#S2.SS4" title="2.4. Kontsevich’s graph complex ‣ 2. Graphs, tropical curves, and moduli ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> and shall not be needed here in the introduction. The graph complex <math id="S1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> has been studied intensively, including in the past few years. See, e.g.,
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">CV03</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">CGV05</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">DRW15</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wil15</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We will construct a quasi-isomorphism from the cellular chain complex computing <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{H}_{*}(\Delta_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{H}_{*}(\Delta_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Passing to homology gives the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S1.SS0.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.SS0.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{H}_{k}(G^{(g)})\xrightarrow{\cong}\widetilde{H}_{2g+k-1}(\Delta_{g};\mathbb{Q})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≅</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{H}_{k}(G^{(g)})\xrightarrow{\cong}\widetilde{H}_{2g+k-1}(\Delta_{g};\mathbb{Q}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Combining Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> then gives a surjection <math id="S1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-6-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})\twoheadrightarrow H_{k}(G^{(g)})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-6-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})\twoheadrightarrow H_{k}(G^{(g)}).</annotation></semantics></math>
In particular, nonvanishing graph homology groups yield nonvanishing results for cohomology of <math id="S1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The full structure of the homology of the graph complex remains mysterious, but several interesting substructures and many nontrivial classes are known and understood. In particular, the linear dual of <math id="S1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{g}H_{0}(G^{(g)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{g}H_{0}(G^{(g)})</annotation></semantics></math> carries a natural Lie bracket, and is isomorphic to the Grothendieck-Teichmüller Lie algebra <math id="S1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{grt}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{grt}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by the main result of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wil15</a>]</cite>. The Lie algebra <math id="S1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{grt}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{grt}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is known to contain a free Lie subalgebra with a generator in each odd degree <math id="S1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bro12</a>]</cite>). These results let us deduce Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">To the best of our knowledge, the only previously known nonvanishing top weight cohomology group on <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr^{W}_{12}H^{6}(\mathcal{M}_{3},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mn>12</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr^{W}_{12}H^{6}(\mathcal{M}_{3},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, which has rank 1 by the work of Looijenga mentioned above <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loo93</a>]</cite>.
Once the general setup of the paper is in place, the result of Looijenga’s computation of this top weight cohomology group can be recovered immediately. It corresponds to the <math id="S1.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional subspace of graph homology spanned by the complete graph on four vertices.
Note in general that the top weight cohomology of <math id="S1.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-tautological and unstable, since stable and tautological classes are of weight equal to their cohomological degree.
Thus, the method presented here probes one piece of the unstable cohomology of <math id="S1.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> that is especially suited to combinatorial study.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">The identification of top weight cohomology of <math id="S1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> with graph homology, provided by Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>, also yields interesting nonvanishing results in degrees other than <math id="S1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4g-6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4g-6</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance, the nontrivial classes in <math id="S1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{3}(G^{(6)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{3}(G^{(6)})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{3}(G^{(8)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{3}(G^{(8)})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{7}(G^{(10)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{7}(G^{(10)})</annotation></semantics></math> discovered by Bar-Natan and McKay <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">BNM</a>]</cite> prove nonvanishing of <math id="S1.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{6};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{6};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{23}(\mathcal{M}_{8};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>23</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{23}(\mathcal{M}_{8};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{27}(\mathcal{M}_{10};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>27</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{27}(\mathcal{M}_{10};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.
It appears that the only previously known example of a nonvanishing odd-degree cohomology group of <math id="S1.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{5}(\mathcal{M}_{4};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{5}(\mathcal{M}_{4};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> which has rank 1 (and weight 6) by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tom05</a>]</cite>. The interest and difficulty in exhibiting odd cohomology classes on <math id="S1.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> was highlighted by Harer and Zagier over three decades ago. They observed that no such classes were known at the time of their writing, and standard methods could produce classes only in even degree, while their Euler characteristic computations showed that such classes are abundant when <math id="S1.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\gg 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\gg 0</annotation></semantics></math> is even: <math id="S1.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)^{g+1}\chi(\mathcal{M}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>χ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)^{g+1}\chi(\mathcal{M}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> grows like <math id="S1.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{2g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{2g}</annotation></semantics></math>. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">HZ86</a>, p. 458]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har88</a>, p. 210]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we may also use the connection between cohomology of <math id="S1.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and graph homology to give an application in the other direction, namely from <math id="S1.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to graph complexes. Using Harer’s computation of the virtual cohomological dimension of <math id="S1.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har86</a>]</cite> and the vanishing of <math id="S1.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-5}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-5}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">CFP12</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">MSS13</a>]</cite>, we give an independent proof of the following recent result of Willwacher <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wil15</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The graph homology groups <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}(G^{(g)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}(G^{(g)})</annotation></semantics></math> vanish for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Relations between graph (co)homology and (co)homology of moduli spaces of curves were also considered by Kontsevich, but the relationships he studied are conceptually quite different. For example, he relates genus <math id="S1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curves to genus <math id="S1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math> graph homology where we relate genus <math id="S1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curves to genus <math id="S1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> graph homology.
The three different Lie algebras mentioned above correspond to three different types of decorations on graphs, and each comes with a corresponding graph complex that computes homology (or cohomology) of an appropriate moduli space of decorated graphs.
The Lie algebra <math id="S1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to graphs decorated with ribbon structure, and moduli spaces of ribbon graphs are homotopy equivalent to moduli spaces of curves with marked points. This is related to the fact that a punctured Riemann surface deformation retracts to a graph, which remembers a ribbon structure from the deformation. The cohomology of <math id="S1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> injects into the cohomology of <math id="S1.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math>, via pullback to the universal curve, and <math id="S1.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is homotopy equivalent to a moduli space of ribbon graphs of first Betti number <math id="S1.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math> that bound exactly <math id="S1.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> open disk. Forgetting the ribbon structure gives a proper map from this moduli space of ribbon graphs to a moduli space of undecorated graphs. The rational homology of the latter space is computed by the graph complex <math id="S1.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(2g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(2g)}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kon93</a>, Section 3]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Here, however, we relate the cohomology of <math id="S1.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to the graph complex <math id="S1.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math>, not <math id="S1.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(2g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(2g)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The graphs appear not as deformation retracts of punctured curves, but rather as dual graphs of stable degenerations. For a (partially conjectural) picture that reproves our main results and relates <math id="S1.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> to ribbon graph complexes, via hairy oriented graph complexes, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">AWŽ20</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p18.1" class="ltx_p">The combinatorial structure of <math id="S1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}^{\mathrm{trop}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}^{\mathrm{trop}}</annotation></semantics></math> is intricately related to both the compactification of <math id="S1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and to the graph complex <math id="S1.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math>. These topological spaces are not strictly needed for proving the main results of this paper; one could instead work in rational chain complexes throughout and define a map directly from <math id="S1.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> to the top weight row of the weight spectral sequence associated to the Deligne-Mumford stable curves compactification of <math id="S1.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently, one may relate <math id="S1.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> to the Feynman transform of the modular operad that associates the vector space <math id="S1.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, with its trivial <math id="S1.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-action, to each pair <math id="S1.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2+n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2+n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">AWŽ20</a>, §6]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Nevertheless, the spaces <math id="S1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{g}^{\mathrm{trop}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{g}^{\mathrm{trop}}</annotation></semantics></math> provide an intuitive way to visualize and motivate the corresponding constructions with chain complexes. Moreover, it is well-known in algebraic topology that maps of spaces carry more information than the induced maps of rational chain complexes or homology groups. In particular the proper map (<a href="#S1.SS0.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.0.1</span></a>) should carry more information than the induced map in compactly supported cohomology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p20.1" class="ltx_p">We note also that our combinatorial topological methods should apply more generally. Any toroidal compactification <math id="S1.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{X}\supset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>⊃</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{X}\supset X</annotation></semantics></math> of a variety or DM stack gives rise to a combinatorial dual complex whose simple homotopy type is independent of the choice of compactification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pay13</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har17</a>]</cite>. The reduced homology of this dual complex computes the top weight cohomology of <math id="S1.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, but the space itself encodes more information. Moreover, if <math id="S1.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{X}</annotation></semantics></math> are moduli spaces and the universal family of <math id="S1.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is a toroidal compactification of that of <math id="S1.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then the dual complex of <math id="S1.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{X}\setminus X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∖</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{X}\setminus X</annotation></semantics></math> typically has a natural interpretation as a tropical moduli space. In this way, one may expect the methods presented here to apply to other moduli spaces, such as moduli of spin curves, moduli of curves with level structure, and moduli of abelian varieties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p21" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acknowledgments.</span> We are grateful to D. Abramovich, E. Getzler, M. Kahle, A. Kupers, L. Migliorini, N. Salter, C. Simpson, O. Tommasi, D. Turchetti, R. Vakil, and K. Vogtmann for helpful conversations related to this work.
MC was supported by NSF
DMS-1204278, DMS-1701924, CAREER DMS-1844768, a Sloan Fellowship and a
Henry Merritt Wriston Fellowship. SG was supported by NSF DMS-1405001 and the European Research Council (ERC) under the European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme (grant agreement No 682922), by the EliteForsk Prize, and by the Danish National Research Foundation (DNRF92 and DNRF151). SP was supported by NSF DMS-1702428 and a Simons Fellowship.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Graphs, tropical curves, and moduli</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we recall in more detail the construction of the topological
space <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as a moduli space for tropical curves, which are marked
weighted graphs with a length assigned to each edge.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Weighted graphs and tropical curves</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite graph, possibly with loops and parallel edges.
All graphs in this paper will be connected. Write <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(G)</annotation></semantics></math>
for the vertex set and edge set, respectively, of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. A <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weighted graph</span> is a connected graph <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> together with a function
<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\colon V(G)\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\colon V(G)\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, called the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">weight function</span>.
The <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">genus</span> of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,w)</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S2.SS1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(G,w)=b_{1}(G)+\sum_{v\in V(G)}\!w(v)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(G,w)=b_{1}(G)+\sum_{v\in V(G)}\!w(v),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}(G)=|E(G)|-|V(G)|+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">−</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}(G)=|E(G)|-|V(G)|+1</annotation></semantics></math> is the first Betti number of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">valence</span> of a vertex <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in a weighted graph, denoted
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\val(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>val</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\val(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, is the number of half-edges of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> incident to <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, a loop edge based at
<math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> counts twice towards <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\val(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>val</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\val(v)</annotation></semantics></math>, once for each end, and an ordinary
edge counts once. We say that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(G,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(G,w)</annotation></semantics></math>
is <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stable</span> if for every <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.SS1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2w(v)-2+\val(v)&gt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>val</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2w(v)-2+\val(v)&gt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, this is equivalent to the condition that every vertex of weight 0
has valence at least 3.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>The category <math id="S2.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The connected
stable graphs of genus <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> form the objects of a
<em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">category</em> which we denote <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. The morphisms in this
category are compositions of contractions of edges <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\rightarrow G/e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\rightarrow G/e</annotation></semantics></math>
and isomorphisms <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\rightarrow G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\rightarrow G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. For the sake of removing any
ambiguity about what that might mean, let us give a formal and precise definition of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, then, a graph <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite set <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(G)=V(G)\sqcup H(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(G)=V(G)\sqcup H(G)</annotation></semantics></math>
(of “vertices” and “half-edges”), together with two functions
<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{G},r_{G}\colon X(G)\to X(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{G},r_{G}\colon X(G)\to X(G)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{G}^{2}=\mathrm{id}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>id</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{G}^{2}=\mathrm{id}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{G}^{2}=r_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{G}^{2}=r_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and that</p>
<table id="S2.SS2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x\in X(G)\mid r_{G}(x)=x\}=\{x\in X(G)\mid s_{G}(x)=x\}=V(G)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x\in X(G)\mid r_{G}(x)=x\}=\{x\in X(G)\mid s_{G}(x)=x\}=V(G).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Informally: <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> sends a half-edge to its other half, while
<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> sends a half-edge to its incident vertex. We let
<math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(G)=H(G)/(x\sim s_{G}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(G)=H(G)/(x\sim s_{G}(x))</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of edges. The definition of
weights, genus, and stability is as before.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The objects of the category <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> are all connected stable graphs of genus <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.
For an object <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}=(G,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}=(G,w)</annotation></semantics></math> we shall write <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>
and similarly for <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝐆</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>𝐆</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then a morphism <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a function <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon X({\bf G})\to X({\bf G}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon X({\bf G})\to X({\bf G}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> with the property that</p>
<table id="S2.SS2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\circ r_{\bf G}=r_{{\bf G}^{\prime}}\circ f\text{ and }f\circ s_{\bf G}=s_{{\bf G}^{\prime}}\circ f," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>𝐆</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝐆</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\circ r_{\bf G}=r_{{\bf G}^{\prime}}\circ f\text{ and }f\circ s_{\bf G}=s_{{\bf G}^{\prime}}\circ f,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and subject to the following three requirements:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in H({\bf G}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in H({\bf G}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> determines the subset
<math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{-1}(e)\subset X({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{-1}(e)\subset X({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> and we require that it consists of
precisely one element (which will then automatically be in
<math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V({\bf G}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V({\bf G}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> determines a subset
<math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{v}=f^{-1}(v)\subset X({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{v}=f^{-1}(v)\subset X({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}_{v}=(S_{v},r|_{S_{v}},s|_{S_{v}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}_{v}=(S_{v},r|_{S_{v}},s|_{S_{v}})</annotation></semantics></math> is a graph; we
require that it be connected and have
<math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\mathbf{S}_{v},w|_{\mathbf{S}_{v}})=w(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\mathbf{S}_{v},w|_{\mathbf{S}_{v}})=w(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Composition of morphisms <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}\to{\bf G}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}\to{\bf G}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in
<math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the corresponding composition
<math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X({\bf G})\to X({\bf G}^{\prime})\to X({\bf G}^{\prime\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X({\bf G})\to X({\bf G}^{\prime})\to X({\bf G}^{\prime\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> in the category of sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our definition of graphs and the morphisms between them is standard in
the study of moduli spaces of curves and agrees, in essence, with the
definitions in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACG11</a>, X.2]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACP15</a>, §3.2]</cite>, as well as
those in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">KM94</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">GK98</a>]</cite>. In particular, our <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> agrees with the category denoted <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\!(g,0)\!)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>,</mo><mpadded style="width:0.219em;" width="0.219em"><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\!(g,0)\!)</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">GK98</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We also note that any morphism <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> can be alternatively
described as an isomorphism following a finite sequence of
<em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">edge collapses</em>: for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in E({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in E({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> there is a morphism
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}\to{\bf G}/e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}\to{\bf G}/e</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}/e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}/e</annotation></semantics></math> is the marked weighted graph
obtained from <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> by collapsing <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> together with its two
endpoints to a single vertex <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[e]\in{\bf G}/e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[e]\in{\bf G}/e</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is not a
loop, the weight of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[e]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[e]</annotation></semantics></math> is the sum of the weights of the endpoints
of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> and if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is a loop the weight of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[e]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[e]</annotation></semantics></math> is one more than the
old weight of the end-point of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\{e_{1},\dots,e_{k}\}\subset E({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\{e_{1},\dots,e_{k}\}\subset E({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> there are iterated edge
collapses <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}\to{\bf G}/e_{1}\to({\bf G}/e_{1})/e_{2}\to\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}\to{\bf G}/e_{1}\to({\bf G}/e_{1})/e_{2}\to\dots</annotation></semantics></math> and any
morphism <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as such an iteration
followed by an isomorphism from the resulting quotient of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> to
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We shall say that <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> have the same <em id="S2.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">combinatorial
type</em> if they are isomorphic in <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact there are only
finitely many isomorphism classes of objects in <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, since any
object has at most <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6g-6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6g-6</annotation></semantics></math> half-edges and <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2</annotation></semantics></math> vertices; and for
each possible set of vertices and half-edges there are finitely many
ways of gluing them to a graph, and finitely many possibilities for
the weight function. In order to get a <em id="S2.SS2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">small</em>
category <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> we shall tacitly pick one object in each
isomorphism class and pass to the full subcategory on those objects.
Hence <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <em id="S2.SS2.p7.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">skeletal</em> category. (Although we shall
usually try to use language compatible with any choice of small
equivalent subcategory <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.) It is clear that all Hom sets in
<math id="S2.SS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> are finite, so <math id="S2.SS2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is in fact a finite category.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Replacing <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> by some choice of skeleton has the effect that if
<math id="S2.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is an object of <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in E(\mathbf{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in E(\mathbf{G})</annotation></semantics></math> is an
edge, then the marked weighted graph <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}/e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}/e</annotation></semantics></math> is likely not equal to
an object of <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a
<em id="S2.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">morphism</em> <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\colon\mathbf{G}\to\mathbf{G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\colon\mathbf{G}\to\mathbf{G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> factoring
through an isomorphism <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}/e\to{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}/e\to{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The pair
<math id="S2.SS2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{G}^{\prime},q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{G}^{\prime},q)</annotation></semantics></math> is unique up to unique isomorphism (but of course the map <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> or the isomorphism
<math id="S2.SS2.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}/e\to{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}/e\to{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on their own need not be unique). By an abuse of notation, we shall henceforward
write <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}/e\in\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}/e\in\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> for the codomain of this unique
morphism, and similarly <math id="S2.SS2.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G/e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G/e</annotation></semantics></math> for its underlying graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let us define a functor</span></p>
<table id="S2.SS2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\colon\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}^{\mathrm{op}}\to(\text{Finite sets},\text{injections})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>op</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Finite sets</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">injections</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\colon\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}^{\mathrm{op}}\to(\text{Finite sets},\text{injections})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">as follows. On objects, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E({\bf G})=E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E({\bf G})=E(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of edges
of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}=(G,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}=(G,w)</annotation></semantics></math> as defined above. A morphism
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> determines an injective function <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(f)</annotation></semantics></math>
sending <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\prime}\in E({\bf G}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\prime}\in E({\bf G}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> to the
unique element <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in E({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in E({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(e)=e^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(e)=e^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This clearly preserves composition and identities, and hence defines
a functor.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Moduli space of tropical curves</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now recall the construction of moduli spaces of stable tropical
curves, as the colimit of a diagram of cones parametrizing possible
lengths of edges for each fixed combinatorial type. The construction
follows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">BMV11</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cap13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fix an integer <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. A <em id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">length function</em> on
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}=(G,w)\in\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}=(G,w)\in\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is an element
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}^{E({\bf G})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}^{E({\bf G})}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we shall think geometrically of
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(e)</annotation></semantics></math> as the <em id="S2.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">length</em> of the edge <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in E({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in E({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math>. A genus <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S2.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">stable tropical curve</em> is then a pair
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma=({\bf G},\ell)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma=({\bf G},\ell)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}\in\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}\in\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}^{E({\bf G})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}^{E({\bf G})}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we shall say that <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bf G},\ell)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bf G},\ell)</annotation></semantics></math> is
<em id="S2.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">isometric</em> to <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bf G}^{\prime},\ell^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bf G}^{\prime},\ell^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> if there exists an isomorphism
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\colon{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\colon{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\prime}=\ell\circ\phi^{-1}\colon E({\bf G}^{\prime})\to\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\prime}=\ell\circ\phi^{-1}\colon E({\bf G}^{\prime})\to\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The
<em id="S2.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">volume</em> of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bf G},\ell)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bf G},\ell)</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S2.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{e\in E({\bf G})}\ell(e)\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{e\in E({\bf G})}\ell(e)\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We can now describe the underlying set of the topological space
<math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the main object of study in this paper. It is
the set of isometry classes of genus <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> stable tropical curves of
volume 1. We proceed to describe its topology and further structure
as a closed subspace of the moduli space of tropical curves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. For each object
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}\in\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}\in\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> define the topological space</span></p>
<table id="S2.SS3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma({\bf G})=\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{E({\bf G})}=\{\ell\colon E({\bf G})\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma({\bf G})=\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{E({\bf G})}=\{\ell\colon E({\bf G})\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a morphism <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> define the continuous map
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma f\colon\sigma({\bf G}^{\prime})\to\sigma({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma f\colon\sigma({\bf G}^{\prime})\to\sigma({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> by</span></p>
<table id="S2.SS3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sigma f)(\ell^{\prime})=\ell\colon E({\bf G})\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sigma f)(\ell^{\prime})=\ell\colon E({\bf G})\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.SS3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(e)=\begin{cases}\ell^{\prime}(e^{\prime})&amp;\text{ if $f$ sends $e$ to $e^{\prime}\in E({\bf G}^{\prime})$},\\
0&amp;\text{ if $f$ collapses $e$ to a vertex}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> if </mtext><mi>f</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> sends </mtext><mi>e</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> to </mtext><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> if </mtext><mi>f</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> collapses </mtext><mi>e</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> to a vertex</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(e)=\begin{cases}\ell^{\prime}(e^{\prime})&amp;\text{ if $f$ sends $e$ to $e^{\prime}\in E({\bf G}^{\prime})$},\\
0&amp;\text{ if $f$ collapses $e$ to a vertex}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This defines a functor
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\colon\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Spaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>op</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Spaces</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\colon\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Spaces}</annotation></semantics></math> and the
topological space <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined to be the colimit of this
functor.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, the topological space <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained as
follows. For each morphism <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the map
<math id="S2.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}\colon\sigma({\bf G}^{\prime})\to\sigma({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}\colon\sigma({\bf G}^{\prime})\to\sigma({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> that sends
<math id="S2.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\prime}\colon E({\bf G}^{\prime})\to\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\prime}\colon E({\bf G}^{\prime})\to\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> to the length function
<math id="S2.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\colon E({\bf G})\to\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\colon E({\bf G})\to\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by extending it
to be 0 on all edges of <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> that are collapsed by <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. So <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>
linearly identifies <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma({\bf G}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma({\bf G}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> with some face of <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math>,
possibly <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> itself. Then</p>
<table id="S2.SS3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}=\left(\coprod\sigma({\bf G})\right)\Big/\{\ell^{\prime}\sim L_{f}(\ell^{\prime})\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∐</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}=\left(\coprod\sigma({\bf G})\right)\Big/\{\ell^{\prime}\sim L_{f}(\ell^{\prime})\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where the equivalence runs over all morphisms <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
and all <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{\prime}\in\sigma({\bf G}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{\prime}\in\sigma({\bf G}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">As we shall explain in more detail in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Symmetric 
        
          Δ
        
      -complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> naturally
comes with more structure than a plain topological space; it is an
example of a <span id="S2.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">generalized cone complex</span>, as defined in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACP15</a>, §2]</cite>. This formalizes the observation that <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is glued
out of the cones <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S2.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">volume</em> defines a function
<math id="S2.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\colon\sigma({\bf G})\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\colon\sigma({\bf G})\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, given explicitly as
<math id="S2.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v(\ell)=\sum_{e\in E({\bf G})}\ell(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v(\ell)=\sum_{e\in E({\bf G})}\ell(e)</annotation></semantics></math>, and for any morphism
<math id="S2.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> the induced map
<math id="S2.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma({\bf G}^{\prime})\to\sigma({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma({\bf G}^{\prime})\to\sigma({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> preserves volume. Hence there is an
induced map <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\colon M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\colon M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and there is a unique element
in <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> with volume 0 which we shall denote <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bullet_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∙</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. The
underlying graph of <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bullet_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∙</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a single vertex with
weight <math id="S2.SS3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the subspace of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizing curves of
volume 1, i.e., the inverse image of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> under
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v:M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v:M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is homeomorphic to the link of <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> at the
cone point <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bullet_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∙</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, it inherits the structure of a
symmetric <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex, as we shall define in
Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Symmetric 
        
          Δ
        
      -complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, from the generalized cone complex structure on
<math id="S2.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\mathrm{trop}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. See Remark <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem15" title="Remark 3.15. ‣ 3.4. Colimit presentations and subdivision ‣ 3. Symmetric 
        
          Δ
        
      -complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.15</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4. </span>Kontsevich’s graph complex</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us briefly recall the (commutative) graph complex, defined by Kontsevich in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kon93</a>]</cite>. This chain complex comes in two versions, differing by some important signs. Kontsevich’s original paper is mostly focused on what he calls the “even” version of the graph complex; it is related to invariants of odd-dimensional manifolds and by Willwacher’s results to deformations of the operad <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for odd <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the same version as considered by e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">CV03</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">CGV05</a>]</cite>. The other version, called “odd” in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kon93</a>]</cite>, is related to invariants of even-dimensional manifolds, deformations of the operad <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for even <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and by the main theorem of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wil15</a>]</cite> to the Grothendieck–Teichmüller Lie algebra. It is the latter version which is relevant to our paper and shall be recalled here. Both are considered in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">BNM</a>]</cite> where they are called the “fundamental example” and the “basic example” of graph homology, respectively (the assertion of op.cit. that the basic example does not occur in nature is of course no longer true).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The graph complex is defined by letting <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> be the rational vector space generated by <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\Gamma,\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\Gamma,\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is a connected graph of genus <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> all of whose vertices have valence at least 3, in other words an object of <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> in which <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(v)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(v)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>. The “orientation” <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> is a total ordering on <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and this notation is subject to the relation <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\Gamma,\omega]=\mathrm{sgn}(\sigma)[\Gamma^{\prime},\omega^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\Gamma,\omega]=\mathrm{sgn}(\sigma)[\Gamma^{\prime},\omega^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> if there exists an isomorphism of graphs <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\cong\Gamma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\cong\Gamma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> under which the total orderings are related by a permutation <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows from this relation that <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\Gamma,\omega]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\Gamma,\omega]=0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> has at least two parallel edges, since then there is an automorphism of <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> inducing an odd permutation of its edge set. The boundary map in this chain complex is induced by</p>
<table id="S2.SS4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.4.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial[\Gamma,\omega]=\sum_{i=0}^{n}(-1)^{i}[\Gamma/e_{i},\omega|_{E(\Gamma/e_{i})}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial[\Gamma,\omega]=\sum_{i=0}^{n}(-1)^{i}[\Gamma/e_{i},\omega|_{E(\Gamma/e_{i})}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=(e_{0}&lt;e_{1}&lt;\dots&lt;e_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=(e_{0}&lt;e_{1}&lt;\dots&lt;e_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the total ordering on the set <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> of edges of <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, the graph <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma/e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma/e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the result of collapsing <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}\subset\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}\subset\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> to a point, and <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega|_{E(\Gamma/e_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega|_{E(\Gamma/e_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math> is the induced ordering on the subset <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\Gamma/e_{i})=E(\Gamma)\setminus\{e_{i}\}\subset E(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\Gamma/e_{i})=E(\Gamma)\setminus\{e_{i}\}\subset E(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>. In case <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}\in E(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}\in E(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> is a loop, we interpret the corresponding term in (<a href="#S2.SS4.E1" title="In 2.4. Kontsevich’s graph complex ‣ 2. Graphs, tropical curves, and moduli ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4.1</span></a>) as zero.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{g}\in G^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{g}\in G^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> be the “wheel graph” with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> trivalent vertices, one <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-valent vertex, and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math> edges arranged in a wheel shape with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> spokes, and with some chosen ordering of its edge set. The graph underlying <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> is depicted in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.4. Kontsevich’s graph complex ‣ 2. Graphs, tropical curves, and moduli ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial W_{g}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial W_{g}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. This gives a non-zero cycle for odd <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, which we also denote <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="78.11" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 81.65 78.11" width="81.65"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,78.11) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(40.83,0) translate(0,35.31)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.42 39.37 C 3.42 41.26 1.89 42.79 0 42.79 C -1.89 42.79 -3.42 41.26 -3.42 39.37 C -3.42 37.48 -1.89 35.95 0 35.95 C 1.89 35.95 3.42 37.48 3.42 39.37 Z M 0 39.37"></path><path style="stroke:none" d="M 40.83 12.2 C 40.83 14.1 39.29 15.63 37.4 15.63 C 35.51 15.63 33.98 14.1 33.98 12.2 C 33.98 10.31 35.51 8.78 37.4 8.78 C 39.29 8.78 40.83 10.31 40.83 12.2 Z M 37.4 12.2"></path><path style="stroke:none" d="M 26.65 -31.89 C 26.65 -30 25.12 -28.46 23.23 -28.46 C 21.34 -28.46 19.8 -30 19.8 -31.89 C 19.8 -33.78 21.34 -35.31 23.23 -35.31 C 25.12 -35.31 26.65 -33.78 26.65 -31.89 Z M 23.23 -31.89"></path><path style="stroke:none" d="M -19.8 -31.89 C -19.8 -30 -21.34 -28.46 -23.23 -28.46 C -25.12 -28.46 -26.65 -30 -26.65 -31.89 C -26.65 -33.78 -25.12 -35.31 -23.23 -35.31 C -21.34 -35.31 -19.8 -33.78 -19.8 -31.89 Z M -23.23 -31.89"></path><path style="stroke:none" d="M -33.98 12.2 C -33.98 14.1 -35.51 15.63 -37.4 15.63 C -39.29 15.63 -40.83 14.1 -40.83 12.2 C -40.83 10.31 -39.29 8.78 -37.4 8.78 C -35.51 8.78 -33.98 10.31 -33.98 12.2 Z M -37.4 12.2"></path><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.42 0 C 3.42 1.89 1.89 3.42 0 3.42 C -1.89 3.42 -3.42 1.89 -3.42 0 C -3.42 -1.89 -1.89 -3.42 0 -3.42 C 1.89 -3.42 3.42 -1.89 3.42 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g stroke-width="1.6pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 35.67 L 0 3.7 M -3.52 1.15 L -33.88 11.06 M -33.88 11.06 L -3.52 1.15 M 2.18 -2.99 L 21.05 -28.9 M 21.05 -28.9 L 2.18 -2.99 M -2.18 -2.99 L -21.05 -28.9 M -21.05 -28.9 L -2.18 -2.99 M 3.52 1.15 L 33.88 11.06"></path></g><g stroke-width="1.6pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 2.99 37.19 L 34.41 14.38 M 36.27 8.68 L 24.36 -28.37 M 19.53 -31.89 L -19.53 -31.89 M -24.36 -28.37 L -36.27 8.68 M -34.41 14.38 L -2.99 37.19"></path></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>The graph <math id="S2.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, any contraction of a single edge <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>, spoke or non-spoke, leads to a graph <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{g}/e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{g}/e</annotation></semantics></math> with two parallel edges, which then represents the zero element in the graph complex.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The automorphism group of <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math> and is isomorphic to the dihedral group <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2g}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;3</annotation></semantics></math>, and it is easy to verify that it acts by even permutations on <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(W_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(W_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is odd. Hence <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{g}\neq 0\in G^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{g}\neq 0\in G^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> for odd <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. (Notice that so far we are only making the elementary claim that it is non-zero on the chain level, although it in fact turns out to represent a non-zero homology class.) On the other hand, the involutions in the dihedral group act by odd permutations on <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(W_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(W_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> for even <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{g}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{g}=0</annotation></semantics></math> in this case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Grading conventions differ from author to author.
In Kontsevich’s original paper, the grading of this chain complex is by number of vertices <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V(\Gamma)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V(\Gamma)|</annotation></semantics></math>. We shall instead use conventions better suited for comparison with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wil15</a>]</cite>, in which the degree of <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V(\Gamma)|-(g+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V(\Gamma)|-(g+1)</annotation></semantics></math>. In this grading the wheel graph has degree 0. As we shall see later, it also has the effect of making <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> a connective chain complex, i.e., its homology vanishes in negative degrees.
Willwacher’s paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wil15</a>]</cite> considers the linearly dual cochain complex which he denotes <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GC}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GC}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GC}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that</p>
<table id="S2.SS4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GC}=\prod_{g=2}^{\infty}\Hom(G^{(g)},\mathbb{Q})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GC}=\prod_{g=2}^{\infty}\Hom(G^{(g)},\mathbb{Q}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom(-,\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom(-,\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the graded dual. For example, for odd <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> there is a cochain <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{g}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{g}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> given by sending the wheel graph <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W_{g},\omega)\mapsto\pm 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">±</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W_{g},\omega)\mapsto\pm 1</annotation></semantics></math> (sign depending on <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>) and any other graph to 0.
In Willwacher’s grading convention, the differential on <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GC}</annotation></semantics></math> raises the degree by <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, the cohomological degree of (the dual basis element corresponding to) <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\Gamma,\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\Gamma,\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> being <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V(\Gamma)|-(g+1)=|E(\Gamma)|-2g." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V(\Gamma)|-(g+1)=|E(\Gamma)|-2g.</annotation></semantics></math>
The differential on <math id="S2.SS4.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GC}</annotation></semantics></math> is then given by precomposing with (<a href="#S2.SS4.E1" title="In 2.4. Kontsevich’s graph complex ‣ 2. Graphs, tropical curves, and moduli ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">As explained in (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wil15</a>, Proposition 3.4]</cite>), <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GC}</annotation></semantics></math> carries a natural combinatorially defined Lie bracket, making it a differential graded Lie algebra.
The main result of Willwacher’s paper gives an isomorphism between the Grothendieck–Teichmüller Lie algebra and graph cohomology in degree 0</p>
<table id="S2.SS4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\mathsf{GC})\cong\mathfrak{grt}_{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\mathsf{GC})\cong\mathfrak{grt}_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p6.2" class="ltx_p">A connected genus <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> graph gives a degree-0 cochain if it has precisely <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math> edges (and hence <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+1</annotation></semantics></math> vertices). Any element of <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\mathsf{GC})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\mathsf{GC})</annotation></semantics></math> may be evaluated on the cycle <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. The dual basis element <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{g}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{g}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> has cohomological degree 0 in <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GC}</annotation></semantics></math>, but is likely not a cocycle.
By definition, the Lie algebra <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{grt}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{grt}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of elements <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> of the completed free Lie algebra on two elements, satisfying certain explicit equations which we shall not recall (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wil15</a>, §6.1]</cite>).
An important consequence of this isomorphism is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wil15</a>]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any odd <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> there exists an element <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{g}\in H^{0}(\mathsf{GC})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{g}\in H^{0}(\mathsf{GC})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\sigma_{g},W_{g}\rangle\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\sigma_{g},W_{g}\rangle\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[W_{g}]\neq 0\in H_{0}(G^{(g)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[W_{g}]\neq 0\in H_{0}(G^{(g)})</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the wheel cycle <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a boundary.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof sketch.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Starting from a suitable Drinfeld associator, Willwacher in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wil15</a>, §9]</cite> translates the corresponding element <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{g}\in\mathfrak{grt}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{g}\in\mathfrak{grt}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GC}</annotation></semantics></math> and proves that the resulting cocycle in <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GC}</annotation></semantics></math> has non-zero coefficient of <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{g}^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{g}^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The group <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}(G^{(g)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}(G^{(g)})</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=5</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 7</annotation></semantics></math>.
Moreover, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim H_{0}(G^{(g)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim H_{0}(G^{(g)})</annotation></semantics></math> grows at least exponentially. More precisely,</span></p>
<table id="S2.SS4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim H_{0}(G^{(g)})&gt;\beta^{g}+\text{constant}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">constant</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim H_{0}(G^{(g)})&gt;\beta^{g}+\text{constant}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for any <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&lt;\beta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&lt;\beta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0}\approx 1.3247\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>1.3247</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0}\approx 1.3247\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> is the real root of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{3}-t-1=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{3}-t-1=0</annotation></semantics></math> .</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> denote the graded <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space generated by symbols <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{2i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{2i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> in degree <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2i+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2i+1</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Lie}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Lie</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Lie}(V)</annotation></semantics></math> be the free Lie algebra on <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.
As explained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wil15</a>]</cite>, the result of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bro12</a>]</cite> implies that the classes <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{2i+1}\in\mathfrak{grt}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{2i+1}\in\mathfrak{grt}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
together with the Lie algebra structure on <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{grt}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{grt}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> gives rise to an injection</span></p>
<table id="S2.SS4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.4.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Lie}(V)\hookrightarrow\mathfrak{grt}_{1}\cong H^{0}(\mathsf{GC})\cong{\bigg(\bigoplus_{g\geq 2}H_{0}(G^{(g)})\bigg)}^{\!\!\raisebox{-2.0pt}{$\scriptstyle\vee$}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Lie</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mpadded voffset="-2.0pt"><mo mathsize="0.700em">∨</mo></mpadded></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Lie}(V)\hookrightarrow\mathfrak{grt}_{1}\cong H^{0}(\mathsf{GC})\cong{\bigg(\bigoplus_{g\geq 2}H_{0}(G^{(g)})\bigg)}^{\!\!\raisebox{-2.0pt}{$\scriptstyle\vee$}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{3},\sigma_{5}\neq 0\in\mathfrak{grt}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{3},\sigma_{5}\neq 0\in\mathfrak{grt}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and since any even number <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math> may be written as <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3+(g-3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3+(g-3)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-3&gt;3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-3&gt;3</annotation></semantics></math>, we also have <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma_{3},\sigma_{g-3}]\neq 0\in\mathfrak{grt}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma_{3},\sigma_{g-3}]\neq 0\in\mathfrak{grt}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which gives rise to a non-zero homomorphism <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}(G^{(g)})\to\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}(G^{(g)})\to\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. More specifically, for <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math> even, <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}(G^{(g)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}(G^{(g)})</annotation></semantics></math> contains a non-zero homology class whose Lie cobracket contains a term <math id="S2.SS4.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{3}\otimes W_{g-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{3}\otimes W_{g-3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the asymptotic statement, we shall compute the Poincaré series (i.e., the generating function for dimension of graded pieces) of <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Lie}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Lie</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Lie}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>, using a variant of Witt’s formula for the dimension of the graded pieces of a free, finitely generated Lie algebra that is generated in degree 1, and then appeal to (<a href="#S2.SS4.E2" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.4. Kontsevich’s graph complex ‣ 2. Graphs, tropical curves, and moduli ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4.2</span></a>). The Poincaré series of <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)=t^{3}/(1-t^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)=t^{3}/(1-t^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. The universal enveloping algebra <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(\mathrm{Lie}(V))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Lie</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(\mathrm{Lie}(V))</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to the free associative algebra <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}V^{\otimes n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{n\geq 0}V^{\otimes n}</annotation></semantics></math>, so has Poincaré series <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/(1-f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(1-f)</annotation></semantics></math>. Now let <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\mathrm{Lie}(V))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Lie</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\mathrm{Lie}(V))</annotation></semantics></math> denote the free commutative <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-algebra on <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Lie}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Lie</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Lie}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>; it has Poincaré series</span></p>
<table id="S2.SS4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{d\geq 0}\frac{1}{(1-t^{d})^{A_{d}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{d\geq 0}\frac{1}{(1-t^{d})^{A_{d}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{d}:=\dim\mathrm{Lie}(V)_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>Lie</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{d}:=\dim\mathrm{Lie}(V)_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are the sought-after coefficients of the Poincaré series for <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Lie}(V)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Lie</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Lie}(V)</annotation></semantics></math>. The Poincaré-Birkhoff-Witt theorem implies that <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U(\mathrm{Lie}(V))\cong S(\mathrm{Lie}(V))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Lie</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Lie</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U(\mathrm{Lie}(V))\cong S(\mathrm{Lie}(V))</annotation></semantics></math> as graded vector spaces, so <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/(1-f)=\prod_{n\geq 0}{1}/{(1-t^{n})^{A_{n}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/(1-f)=\prod_{n\geq 0}{1}/{(1-t^{n})^{A_{n}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\tfrac{d}{dt}\operatorname{log}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\tfrac{d}{dt}\operatorname{log}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to both sides yields</span></p>
<table id="S2.SS4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2.4.3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(t):=\frac{t^{3}(3-t^{2})}{(1-t^{2})(1-t^{2}-t^{3})}=\sum_{d\geq 0}dA_{d}\,\frac{t^{d}}{1-t^{d}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(t):=\frac{t^{3}(3-t^{2})}{(1-t^{2})(1-t^{2}-t^{3})}=\sum_{d\geq 0}dA_{d}\,\frac{t^{d}}{1-t^{d}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text">Write <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(t)=\sum_{n\geq 0}a_{n}t^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(t)=\sum_{n\geq 0}a_{n}t^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. To analyze the <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, notice that <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(t)</annotation></semantics></math> has five simple poles, at the roots of <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-t^{2})(1-t^{2}-t^{3})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-t^{2})(1-t^{2}-t^{3})=0</annotation></semantics></math>. There is a unique root <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\approx 0.75488\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>0.75488</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\approx 0.75488\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> having smallest absolute value, and <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Res}_{\alpha}p(t)=-\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Res</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Res}_{\alpha}p(t)=-\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> (the exact value of the residue is not important). Therefore <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m21" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(t)=-\alpha/(t-\alpha)+\sum_{n\geq 0}b_{n}t^{n}=\sum_{n\geq 0}(\tfrac{1}{\alpha^{n}}+b_{n})t^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(t)=-\alpha/(t-\alpha)+\sum_{n\geq 0}b_{n}t^{n}=\sum_{n\geq 0}(\tfrac{1}{\alpha^{n}}+b_{n})t^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{n\geq 0}b_{n}t^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{n\geq 0}b_{n}t^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> converges on a disc centered at <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> of radius <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{n}\alpha^{n}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{n}\alpha^{n}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}\alpha^{n}=(\tfrac{1}{\alpha^{n}}+b_{n})\alpha^{n}\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}\alpha^{n}=(\tfrac{1}{\alpha^{n}}+b_{n})\alpha^{n}\to 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Setting <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0}=1/\alpha\approx 1.32472\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>1.32472</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0}=1/\alpha\approx 1.32472\ldots</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.SS4.p9.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}\to\beta_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}\to\beta_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now equating coefficients in (<a href="#S2.SS4.E3" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.4. Kontsevich’s graph complex ‣ 2. Graphs, tropical curves, and moduli ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4.3</span></a>) yields <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}=\sum_{d|n}dA_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}=\sum_{d|n}dA_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, so</span></p>
<table id="S2.SS4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.SS4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{d|n}\mu\!\left(\frac{n}{d}\right)a_{d}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.433em;" width="0.433em"><mi>μ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{d|n}\mu\!\left(\frac{n}{d}\right)a_{d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS4.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">by Möbius inversion. Since <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> grows exponentially, the summand when <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=n</annotation></semantics></math>, namely <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}/n</annotation></semantics></math>, eventually dominates the other terms in the sum, and <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> grows faster than <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S2.SS4.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&lt;\beta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&lt;\beta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">While the exponential growth rate in Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 2.7. ‣ 2.4. Kontsevich’s graph complex ‣ 2. Graphs, tropical curves, and moduli ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> relies crucially on the main results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wil15</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bro12</a>]</cite>, there are easier and more direct proofs of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 2.6 ([]). ‣ 2.4. Kontsevich’s graph complex ‣ 2. Graphs, tropical curves, and moduli ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>, constructing an element of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\mathsf{GC})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\mathsf{GC})</annotation></semantics></math> detecting <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[W_{g}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[W_{g}]</annotation></semantics></math> using configuration space integrals as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx47" title="" class="ltx_ref">RW14</a>, §6]</cite>. This nonvanishing of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[W_{g}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[W_{g}]</annotation></semantics></math> for odd <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> is sufficient for disproving <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kon93</a>, Conjecture 7C]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">CFP14</a>, Conjecture 9]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Symmetric <math id="S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes</h2>

<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0em">≥</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq-1</annotation></semantics></math> an integer, we set</span></p>
<table id="S3.SS0.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.SS0.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]=\{0,\ldots,p\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]=\{0,\ldots,p\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This notation includes <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1]=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1]=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> by convention.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As usual we write <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{p}\subset\mathbb{R}^{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{p}\subset\mathbb{R}^{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math> for the standard simplex, i.e., the convex hull of the standard basis vectors <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{0},\dots,e_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{0},\dots,e_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>; its points are <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=(t_{0},\dots,t_{p})=\sum t_{i}e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=(t_{0},\dots,t_{p})=\sum t_{i}e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum t_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum t_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Associating the standard simplex
<math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> to the number <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> may be promoted to a functor from finite sets to
topological spaces; for a finite set <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> define
<math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Delta^{S}=\{a\colon S\to[0,\infty)\mid\sum a(s)=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mo>∑</mo><mi>a</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{S}=\{a\colon S\to[0,\infty)\mid\sum a(s)=1\}</annotation></semantics></math> in the
Euclidean topology and for any map of finite sets <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\colon S\to T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\colon S\to T</annotation></semantics></math>,
define <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{*}\colon\Delta^{S}\to\Delta^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{*}\colon\Delta^{S}\to\Delta^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S3.SS0.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.SS0.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\theta_{*}a)(t)=\sum_{\theta(s)=t}a(s)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\theta_{*}a)(t)=\sum_{\theta(s)=t}a(s).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The usual <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-simplex is recovered as <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{p}=\Delta^{[p]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{p}=\Delta^{[p]}</annotation></semantics></math> with
<math id="S3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]=\{0,\dots,p\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]=\{0,\dots,p\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Recollections on <math id="S3.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us write <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}\in\Delta^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}\in\Delta^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th vertex, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq i\leq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq i\leq p</annotation></semantics></math>. We order the set of vertices in <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{0}&lt;\dots&lt;e_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{0}&lt;\dots&lt;e_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathrm{inj}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>inj</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathrm{inj}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the category with one object
<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]=\{0,\dots,p\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]=\{0,\dots,p\}</annotation></semantics></math> for each integer <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, in which the
morphisms <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]\to[q]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]\to[q]</annotation></semantics></math> are the order preserving injective maps. We
shall take the following as the official definition of a
<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex (sometimes known as “semi-simplicial set” in the more recent literature).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex
is a functor <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\colon\Delta_{\mathrm{inj}}^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>inj</mi><mi>op</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\colon\Delta_{\mathrm{inj}}^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">geometric realization</em> of a <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S3.SS1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.1.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.SS1.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|X|=\Big(\coprod_{p=0}^{\infty}X([p])\times\Delta^{p}\Big)\big/\sim," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∐</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∼</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X|=\Big(\coprod_{p=0}^{\infty}X([p])\times\Delta^{p}\Big)\big/\sim,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math> is the equivalence relation generated by <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,\theta_{*}a)\sim(\theta^{*}x,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,\theta_{*}a)\sim(\theta^{*}x,a)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X([q])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X([q])</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\colon[p]\to[q]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\colon[p]\to[q]</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathrm{inj}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>inj</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathrm{inj}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Delta^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Delta^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each element <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X([p])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X([p])</annotation></semantics></math> determines a map of topological spaces <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\colon\Delta^{p}\to|X|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\colon\Delta^{p}\to|X|</annotation></semantics></math>, and the functor <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\colon\Delta_{\mathrm{inj}}^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>inj</mi><mi>op</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\colon\Delta_{\mathrm{inj}}^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}</annotation></semantics></math> may be recovered from the topological space <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X|</annotation></semantics></math> together with this set of maps from simplices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As is customary, we shall usually write <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}=X([p])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}=X([p])</annotation></semantics></math>.
We also write <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{i}\colon[p-1]\to[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{i}\colon[p-1]\to[p]</annotation></semantics></math> for the unique order preserving injective map whose image does not contain <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}\colon X_{p}\to X_{p-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}\colon X_{p}\to X_{p-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for the induced map.
There is also a category of <em id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">augmented</em> <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes, which are functors <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta_{\mathrm{inj}}\cup\{[-1]\})^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>inj</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>op</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta_{\mathrm{inj}}\cup\{[-1]\})^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1]=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1]=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> is added to <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathrm{inj}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>inj</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathrm{inj}}</annotation></semantics></math> as initial object. The geometric realization <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X|</annotation></semantics></math> then comes with a continuous map <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\colon|X|\to X_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\colon|X|\to X_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Symmetric <math id="S3.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The notion of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes may be generalized to
allow, in their geometric realizations, gluing along maps <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{q}\to\Delta^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{q}\to\Delta^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> that do not preserve the ordering of the vertices. This includes gluing along maps from <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> to itself induced by permuting the vertices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> be the category with the same objects as
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathrm{inj}}\cup\{[-1]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>inj</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathrm{inj}}\cup\{[-1]\}</annotation></semantics></math>, but whose morphisms <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]\to[q]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]\to[q]</annotation></semantics></math> are
all injective maps <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,\dots,p\}\to\{0,\dots,q\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,\dots,p\}\to\{0,\dots,q\}</annotation></semantics></math>. A
<em id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">symmetric <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex</em> (or symmetric semi-simplicial set) is a functor
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\colon I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>op</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\colon I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Such a functor is given by a set <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> for each
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0em">≥</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq-1</annotation></semantics></math>, actions of the symmetric group <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, and face maps <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}\colon X_{p}\to X_{p-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}\colon X_{p}\to X_{p-1}</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq i\leq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq i\leq p</annotation></semantics></math>. The face maps satisfy the usual simplicial
identities as well as a compatibility with the symmetric group action.
We have chosen the name in analogy with the
“symmetric simplicial sets” in the literature (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gra01</a>]</cite>), which is a similar notion also including degeneracy maps.
The <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">geometric realization</em> of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is given by
formula (<a href="#S3.SS1.E1" title="In 3.1. Recollections on 
          
            Δ
          
        -complexes ‣ 3. Symmetric 
        
          Δ
        
      -complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1.1</span></a>), where the equivalence relation now
uses all morphisms <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Symmetric <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes also come with a set <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{-1}=X(\emptyset)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{-1}=X(\emptyset)</annotation></semantics></math> and there is an augmentation map <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X|\to X_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X|\to X_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. (So strictly speaking “augmented symmetric <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes” would be a more accurate name, but we use “symmetric <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes” for brevity.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">standard orthant</em> <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{[p]}=\prod_{i=0}^{p}[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{[p]}=\prod_{i=0}^{p}[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> is functorial in <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]\in I</annotation></semantics></math> by letting <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in I([p],[q])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in I([p],[q])</annotation></semantics></math> act as <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{*}(t_{0},\dots,t_{p})=\sum t_{i}e_{\theta(i)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{*}(t_{0},\dots,t_{p})=\sum t_{i}e_{\theta(i)}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{[p]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{[p]}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th standard basis vector. Replacing <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> by the standard orthant in the definition of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X|</annotation></semantics></math> we arrive at the <em id="S3.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cone over</em> <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.SS2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.2.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.SS2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="CX=\Big(\coprod_{p={-1}}^{\infty}X_{p}\times\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{[p]}\Big)\big/\sim," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∐</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∼</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CX=\Big(\coprod_{p={-1}}^{\infty}X_{p}\times\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{[p]}\Big)\big/\sim,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math> is the equivalence relation generated by <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,\theta_{*}a)\sim(\theta^{*}x,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,\theta_{*}a)\sim(\theta^{*}x,a)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q\geq-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0em">≥</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q\geq-1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{[p]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{[p]}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in I([p],[q])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in I([p],[q])</annotation></semantics></math>. The maps <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\colon\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{[p]}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\colon\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{[p]}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> given by <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{0},\dots,t_{p})\mapsto\sum t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{0},\dots,t_{p})\mapsto\sum t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are compatible with this gluing, and induce a canonical map</p>
<table id="S3.SS2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.SS2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{X}\colon CX\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{X}\colon CX\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">so that <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\mapsto CX" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\mapsto CX</annotation></semantics></math> naturally takes values in the category of spaces over <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have canonical homeomorphisms <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{X}^{-1}(1)=|X|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{X}^{-1}(1)=|X|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{X}^{-1}(0)=X_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{X}^{-1}(0)=X_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and from <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{X}^{-1}([0,1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{X}^{-1}([0,1])</annotation></semantics></math> to the mapping cone of the augmentation <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X|\to X_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X|\to X_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The inclusions <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{-1}\subset\ell_{X}^{-1}([0,1])\subset CX" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{-1}\subset\ell_{X}^{-1}([0,1])\subset CX</annotation></semantics></math> are both deformation retractions. It follows that the quotient <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CX/|X|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CX/|X|</annotation></semantics></math> deformation retracts to the mapping cone of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X|\to X_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X|\to X_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{-1}=\{\ast\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{-1}=\{\ast\}</annotation></semantics></math> the space <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CX" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CX</annotation></semantics></math> deformation retracts to the cone over <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X|</annotation></semantics></math> (hence the name) and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CX/|X|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CX/|X|</annotation></semantics></math> deformation retracts to the unreduced suspension <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S|X|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S|X|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The representable functor <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(-,[p])\colon I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>op</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(-,[p])\colon I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}</annotation></semantics></math> has geometric realization <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|I(-,[p])|\cong\Delta^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|I(-,[p])|\cong\Delta^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">An (abstract) simplicial complex <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> with vertex set <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> determines
a symmetric <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}\colon I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>op</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}\colon I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}</annotation></semantics></math>, sending <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]</annotation></semantics></math> to the set of injective maps <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\colon[p]\to V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\colon[p]\to V</annotation></semantics></math> whose image spans a simplex of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. The realizations of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> as a simplicial complex and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> as a symmetric <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex are canonically homeomorphic.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A typical example of a symmetric <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex in which the symmetric groups do not act freely is the half interval given as a coequalizer of the two distinct morphisms</span></p>
<table id="S3.SS2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.SS2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\mathrm{colim}(I(-,[1])\genfrac{.}{.}{0.0pt}{1}{\raise-2pt\hbox{$\scriptstyle\longrightarrow$}}{\raise 4pt\hbox{$\scriptstyle\longrightarrow$}}I(-,[1]))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>colim</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0.0pt"><mpadded voffset="-2.0pt"><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">⟶</mo></mpadded><mpadded voffset="4.0pt"><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">⟶</mo></mpadded></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\mathrm{colim}(I(-,[1])\genfrac{.}{.}{0.0pt}{1}{\raise-2pt\hbox{$\scriptstyle\longrightarrow$}}{\raise 4pt\hbox{$\scriptstyle\longrightarrow$}}I(-,[1])),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are one-element
sets and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. The unique element in
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> gives a map <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{1}\to|X|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{1}\to|X|</annotation></semantics></math> which is not injective; it
identifies <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X|</annotation></semantics></math> with the topological quotient of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by the
action of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> that reverses the orientation of the interval.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Cellular chains</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rational homology of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X|</annotation></semantics></math> and the relative homology of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(CX,|X|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(CX,|X|)</annotation></semantics></math> may be calculated by cellular chain complexes, functorially associated to <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X:I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>op</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X:I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be a commutative ring and write <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RX_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RX_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> for the free <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module spanned by the set <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. The group of cellular <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-chains <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{p}(X;R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{p}(X;R)</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S3.SS3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{p}(X;R)=(R^{\mathrm{sgn}}\otimes_{RS_{p+1}}RX_{p})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>sgn</mi></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{p}(X;R)=(R^{\mathrm{sgn}}\otimes_{RS_{p+1}}RX_{p})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\mathrm{sgn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>sgn</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\mathrm{sgn}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the action of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> via the sign.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The boundary map <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial:C_{p}(X)\to C_{p-1}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial:C_{p}(X)\to C_{p-1}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique map that makes the following diagram commute:</p>
<table id="S3.SS3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS3.Ex2.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="82.16" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-55.38640616757256px" version="1.1" viewBox="32.83 55.39 219.11 82.16" width="219.11"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 32.825947665600786 82.16132658455273) translate(0,3.46) translate(32.83,0)"><g transform="translate(-18.93,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.14em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.41" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="29.56"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex2.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{{RX_{p}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{{RX_{p}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 18.930357324313338 -0.27673578005007266 L 18.930357324313338 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(38.46,0) translate(0,13.39) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,0.45)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.12em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.7em;font-size:10pt;" height="18.47" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.79)" width="57.08"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex2.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\sum(-1)^{i}(d_{i})_{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\sum(-1)^{i}(d_{i})_{*}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(123.36,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 18.930357324313338 -0.27673578005007266 L 123.36316075053834 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 123.36316075053834 -0.27673578005007266 L 123.36316075053834 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -11.569253138458645 L 0.27673578005007266 -11.569253138458645" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(0,-32.75)"><g transform="translate(0,4.15)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -32.74755291841791 L 0.27673578005007266 -11.569253138458645" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -32.74755291841791 L 0.27673578005007266 -32.74755291841791" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(123.36,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.8em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.41" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="38.8"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex2.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{{RX_{p-1}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{{RX_{p-1}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 146.63873643190252 -11.569253138458645 L 147.19220799200264 -11.569253138458645" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(146.92,0) translate(0,-21.91) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-2.09)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.47em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="6.47"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex2.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(146.92,0) translate(0,-32.75)"><g transform="translate(0,4.15)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 146.63873643190252 -32.74755291841791 L 147.19220799200264 -11.569253138458645" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 146.63873643190252 -32.74755291841791 L 147.19220799200264 -32.74755291841791" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(-32.83,0) translate(0,-43.82) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4.14em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="57.35"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex2.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{{C_{p}(X;R)}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{{C_{p}(X;R)}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 32.825947665600786 -44.093888809163985 L 32.825947665600786 -43.54041724906384" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(63.08,0) translate(0,-36.3) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.36)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.43em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.73" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="5.91"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex2.m1.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\partial}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.700em">∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\partial}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(107.55,0) translate(0,-43.82)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 32.825947665600786 -44.093888809163985 L 107.54574841279879 -43.54041724906384" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 107.54574841279879 -44.093888809163985 L 107.54574841279879 -43.54041724906384" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(107.55,0) translate(0,-43.82) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.09em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="14.34" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="70.44"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex2.m1.pic1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{C_{p-1}(X;R).}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{C_{p-1}(X;R).}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">It is easily verified that such a homomorphism <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∂</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial</annotation></semantics></math> exists, and satisfies <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\partial^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, we define cochains</p>
<table id="S3.SS3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{p}(X;R)=\Hom{Z}(C_{p}(X;\mathbb{Z}),R)=\Hom_{RS_{p+1}}(RX_{p},R^{\mathrm{sgn}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>sgn</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{p}(X;R)=\Hom{Z}(C_{p}(X;\mathbb{Z}),R)=\Hom_{RS_{p+1}}(RX_{p},R^{\mathrm{sgn}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">with coboundary <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta=(-1)^{p+1}\partial^{\vee}\colon C^{p}(X;R)\to C^{p+1}(X;R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.278em">∂</mo><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=(-1)^{p+1}\partial^{\vee}\colon C^{p}(X;R)\to C^{p+1}(X;R)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In other words, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{p}(X;R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{p}(X;R)</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-module consisting
of all set maps <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\colon X_{p}\to R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\colon X_{p}\to R</annotation></semantics></math> which satisfy
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\sigma x)=\mathrm{sgn}(\sigma)\phi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\sigma x)=\mathrm{sgn}(\sigma)\phi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> for all
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and all <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in S_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in S_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">To compare this cellular chain complex to singular homology, write <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{p}\in C_{p}^{\mathrm{sing}}(\Delta^{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ι</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>sing</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{p}\in C_{p}^{\mathrm{sing}}(\Delta^{p})</annotation></semantics></math> for the chain given by the identity map of <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota_{p}^{\prime}\in C_{p}^{\mathrm{sing}}(\Delta^{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>sing</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota_{p}^{\prime}\in C_{p}^{\mathrm{sing}}(\Delta^{p})</annotation></semantics></math> for its barycentric subdivision (in the sense of e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hat02</a>, p. 122]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bre93</a>, §IV.17]</cite>). Any element <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> gives a map <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\colon\Delta^{p}\to|X|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\colon\Delta^{p}\to|X|</annotation></semantics></math>, and we define a natural transformation</p>
<table id="S3.SS3.E1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.SS3.E1X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(3.3.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.SS3.E1X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C_{p}(X;\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C_{p}(X;\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.SS3.E1X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\to C_{p}^{\mathrm{sing}}(|X|;\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>sing</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\to C_{p}^{\mathrm{sing}}(|X|;\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.SS3.E1Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.SS3.E1Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.SS3.E1Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mapsto x_{*}(\iota_{p}^{\prime})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mapsto x_{*}(\iota_{p}^{\prime}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.3" class="ltx_p">This is well defined because <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota^{\prime}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota^{\prime}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{*}(\iota_{p}^{\prime})=\mathrm{sgn}(\sigma)\iota_{p}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ι</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{*}(\iota_{p}^{\prime})=\mathrm{sgn}(\sigma)\iota_{p}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in S_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in S_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and a defines a chain homomorphism <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{*}(X;\mathbb{Z})_{\geq 0}\to C_{*}^{\mathrm{sing}}(|X|;\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo><mi>sing</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{*}(X;\mathbb{Z})_{\geq 0}\to C_{*}^{\mathrm{sing}}(|X|;\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> where we write <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{*}(X;\mathbb{Z})_{\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{*}(X;\mathbb{Z})_{\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> for the quotient by <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}X_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}X_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. By applying <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\otimes{Z}(-)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\otimes{Z}(-)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Hom{Z}(-,R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Hom{Z}(-,R)</annotation></semantics></math> to (<a href="#S3.SS3.E1" title="In 3.3. Cellular chains ‣ 3. Symmetric 
        
          Δ
        
      -complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3.1</span></a>) we obtain natural natural transformations of chains and cochains with coefficients in <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The homomorphisms</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.SS3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle H_{p}(C_{*}(X;R)_{\geq 0},\partial)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{p}(C_{*}(X;R)_{\geq 0},\partial)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\to H_{p}^{\mathrm{sing}}(|X|;R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>sing</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\to H_{p}^{\mathrm{sing}}(|X|;R)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.SS3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H^{p}(C^{*}(X;R)_{\geq 0},\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H^{p}(C^{*}(X;R)_{\geq 0},\delta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leftarrow H^{p}_{\mathrm{sing}}(|X|;R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>sing</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leftarrow H^{p}_{\mathrm{sing}}(|X|;R)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">induced by the (co)chain homomorphisms defined above are isomorphisms, provided
the orders of stabilizers of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> are invertible in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., if the actions are all free or if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}\subset R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}\subset R</annotation></semantics></math>). Under this assumption, there are induced isomorphisms</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.SS3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle H_{p}(C_{*}(X;R),\partial)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{p}(C_{*}(X;R),\partial)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\cong H^{\mathrm{sing}}_{p+1}(CX,|X|;R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>sing</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\cong H^{\mathrm{sing}}_{p+1}(CX,|X|;R)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.SS3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H^{p}(C_{*}(X;R),\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H^{p}(C_{*}(X;R),\delta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\cong H_{\mathrm{sing}}^{p+1}(CX,|X|;R)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>sing</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\cong H_{\mathrm{sing}}^{p+1}(CX,|X|;R).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a singleton, the right hand sides here are reduced homology and cohomology <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{H}_{p}^{\mathrm{sing}}(|X|;R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi><mi>sing</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{H}_{p}^{\mathrm{sing}}(|X|;R)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{H}^{p}_{\mathrm{sing}}(|X|;R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>sing</mi><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{H}^{p}_{\mathrm{sing}}(|X|;R)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">The symmetric <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is filtered by subcomplexes <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{(p)}\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{(p)}\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> defined by setting <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{(p)}_{q}=X_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{(p)}_{q}=X_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\leq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\leq p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{(p)}_{q}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{(p)}_{q}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&gt;p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&gt;p</annotation></semantics></math>. The quotient space <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X^{(p)}|/|X^{(p-1)}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X^{(p)}|/|X^{(p-1)}|</annotation></semantics></math> may be identified with the orbit space</span></p>
<table id="S3.SS3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X^{(p)}|/|X^{(p-1)}|\cong\bigg(\frac{X_{p}\times\Delta^{p}}{X_{p}\times\partial\Delta^{p}}\bigg)/S_{p+1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">×</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X^{(p)}|/|X^{(p-1)}|\cong\bigg(\frac{X_{p}\times\Delta^{p}}{X_{p}\times\partial\Delta^{p}}\bigg)/S_{p+1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">and the induced map</span></p>
<table id="S3.SS3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\mathrm{sgn}}\otimes_{RS_{p+1}}RX_{p}\to H^{\mathrm{sing}}_{p}((X_{p}\times\Delta^{p})/S_{p+1},(X_{p}\times\partial\Delta^{p})/S_{p+1};R)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>sgn</mi></msup><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>sing</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.055em">×</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\mathrm{sgn}}\otimes_{RS_{p+1}}RX_{p}\to H^{\mathrm{sing}}_{p}((X_{p}\times\Delta^{p})/S_{p+1},(X_{p}\times\partial\Delta^{p})/S_{p+1};R)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">is an isomorphism under the assumption. Now proceed by induction on skeleta, using the five-lemma and the long exact sequences associated to the pairs <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X^{(p)},X^{(p-1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X^{(p)},X^{(p-1)})</annotation></semantics></math>, exactly as in the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hat02</a>, Theorem 2.2.27]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the augmented statement use <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X|\to X_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X|\to X_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> to add one more term to the singular chain complex</span></p>
<table id="S3.SS3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dots\to C^{\mathrm{sing}}_{1}(|X|;R)\to C^{\mathrm{sing}}_{0}(|X|;R)\to RX_{-1}\to 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>sing</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>sing</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dots\to C^{\mathrm{sing}}_{1}(|X|;R)\to C^{\mathrm{sing}}_{0}(|X|;R)\to RX_{-1}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">This complex calculates <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\mathrm{sing}}_{*+1}(CX,|X|;R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>sing</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\mathrm{sing}}_{*+1}(CX,|X|;R)</annotation></semantics></math>,
since the inclusion <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{-1}\subset CX" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{-1}\subset CX</annotation></semantics></math> is a deformation retraction. The claim is now easily deduced from the absolute case, and cohomology is similar.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Henceforth we shall use the same notation <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}(-;R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}(-;R)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(-;R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(-;R)</annotation></semantics></math> for the singular and cellular theories.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a symmetric <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> define</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.SS3.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle H_{p}(X;R)=H_{p}(C_{*}(X;R),\partial)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo>∂</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{p}(X;R)=H_{p}(C_{*}(X;R),\partial)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.SS3.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H^{p}(X;R)=H^{p}(C^{*}(X;R),\delta)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H^{p}(X;R)=H^{p}(C^{*}(X;R),\delta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a singleton these agree with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{H}_{p}(|X|;R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{H}_{p}(|X|;R)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{H}^{p}(|X|;R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{H}^{p}(|X|;R)</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, provided orders of stabilizers of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> are invertible in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In practice the cellular homology may be studied using the following observation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.10</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a symmetric <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex, and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> be a commutative ring. Suppose we are given subsets <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{p}\subset X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{p}\subset X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, and suppose that either</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the induced map </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{p+1}\times T_{p}\to X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{p+1}\times T_{p}\to X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a bijection for all </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, or</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}\subset R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}\subset R</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the composition </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{p}\to X_{p}\to X_{p}/S_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{p}\to X_{p}\to X_{p}/S_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is injective, the stabilizer of any </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in T_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in T_{p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is contained in </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and any point </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> whose stabilizer is contained in </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is in the </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-orbit of some </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}\in T_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}\in T_{p}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then the map</span></p>
<table id="S3.SS3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.SS3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RT_{p}\to C_{p}(X;R)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RT_{p}\to C_{p}(X;R)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is an isomorphism of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>-modules.∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{*}(|X|;\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{*}(|X|;\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> may be calculated from a chain complex with one generator for each element in a set of representatives for orbits of elements with alternating stabilizers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.11</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A similar construction was used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">HV98</a>]</cite> to find
a small model for the rational chains of a certain space, except
that instead of our <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{p}/H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{p}/H</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H&lt;S_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H&lt;S_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math> their basic
building blocks are of the form <math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]^{n}/H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]^{n}/H</annotation></semantics></math> for certain subgroups
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of the symmetry group of a cube.
There is also a general construction used by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ber99</a>]</cite>,
in which simplices are replaced by polysimplicial sets.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Colimit presentations and subdivision</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Any symmetric <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex is isomorphic to the colimit of a diagram consisting of representable functors <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(-,[p])\colon I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>op</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(-,[p])\colon I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}</annotation></semantics></math> and morphisms between them. This is a special case of a general fact about presheaves of sets on a small category, cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx42" title="" class="ltx_ref">ML98</a>, §III.7]</cite>, but let us recall how it works in our case.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\colon I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>op</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\colon I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}</annotation></semantics></math>, define a category <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> whose objects are pairs <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="([p],x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">([p],x)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X([p])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X([p])</annotation></semantics></math> and whose morphisms <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="([p],x)\to([p^{\prime}],x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">([p],x)\to([p^{\prime}],x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> are the <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in I([p^{\prime}],[p])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in I([p^{\prime}],[p])</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(\theta)(x)=x^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(\theta)(x)=x^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. For later use we point out that <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism in <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=p^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=p^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that there is a canonical morphism of symmetric <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes</p>
<table id="S3.SS4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3.4.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.SS4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\colim_{([p],x)\in J_{X}}I(-,[p])\to X," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>colim</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\colim_{([p],x)\in J_{X}}I(-,[p])\to X,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">assembled from the morphisms <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\colon I(-,[p])\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\colon I(-,[p])\to X</annotation></semantics></math>. Using that colimits in the category of symmetric <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes are calculated object-wise, it is easy to verify that this morphism is in fact always an isomorphism.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We sometimes use this to reduce a statement about all symmetric <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes to a statement about representable ones, when the statement is preserved by taking colimits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.12</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The functor <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\mapsto|X|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\mapsto|X|</annotation></semantics></math> from symmetric <math id="S3.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes to topological spaces preserves all small colimits.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By (<a href="#S3.SS4.E1" title="In 3.4. Colimit presentations and subdivision ‣ 3. Symmetric 
        
          Δ
        
      -complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4.1</span></a>), the geometric realization functor is characterized by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem12" title="Lemma 3.12. ‣ 3.4. Colimit presentations and subdivision ‣ 3. Symmetric 
        
          Δ
        
      -complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.12</span></a> and the homeomorphisms <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|I(-,[p])|=\Delta^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|I(-,[p])|=\Delta^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, natural in <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]\in I</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly for <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\mapsto CX" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\mapsto CX</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">For a topological space <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> we let <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Sing}(Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Sing</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Sing}(Z)</annotation></semantics></math> be the
symmetric <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex which sends <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]</annotation></semantics></math> to the set of all
continuous maps <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{p}\to Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{p}\to Z</annotation></semantics></math>. It is easily verified that the resulting functor <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Sing}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Sing</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Sing}</annotation></semantics></math> is right adjoint to the geometric realization functor, which therefore preserves all small colimits (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx42" title="" class="ltx_ref">ML98</a>, V.5]</cite>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let us briefly discuss a barycentric subdivision functor, from symmetric <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes to (augmented) <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes.
We first define the barycentric subdivision of the symmetric <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(-,[p])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(-,[p])</annotation></semantics></math> by sending <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[q]\in\Delta_{\mathrm{inj}}\cup\{[-1]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>inj</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[q]\in\Delta_{\mathrm{inj}}\cup\{[-1]\}</annotation></semantics></math> to the set of all flags of subsets <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\emptyset\subsetneq A_{0}\subsetneq\dots\subsetneq A_{q}\subseteq[p])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊊</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⊊</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\emptyset\subsetneq A_{0}\subsetneq\dots\subsetneq A_{q}\subseteq[p])</annotation></semantics></math>. The subdivision <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sd}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sd}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> of a general <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\colon I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>op</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\colon I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}</annotation></semantics></math> is then
defined as the colimit in augmented <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes</p>
<table id="S3.SS4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.SS4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sd}(X)=\colim_{([p],x)\in J_{X}}\mathrm{sd}(I(-,[p]))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>sd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>colim</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>sd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sd}(X)=\colim_{([p],x)\in J_{X}}\mathrm{sd}(I(-,[p])).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Explicitly, this spells out to the formula</p>
<table id="S3.SS4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.SS4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{sd}(X)([q])=\big(\coprod_{p}X_{p}\times\mathrm{sd}(I(-,[p]))_{q}\big)/\sim," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sd</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∐</mo><mi>p</mi></munder><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>sd</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∼</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sd}(X)([q])=\big(\coprod_{p}X_{p}\times\mathrm{sd}(I(-,[p]))_{q}\big)/\sim,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math> is the equivalence relation generated by
<math id="S3.SS4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,\theta^{*}b)\sim(X(\theta)(x),b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,\theta^{*}b)\sim(X(\theta)(x),b)</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\mathrm{sd}(I(-,[p^{\prime}]))_{q}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>sd</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\mathrm{sd}(I(-,[p^{\prime}]))_{q},</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in I([p^{\prime}],[p])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in I([p^{\prime}],[p])</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Equivalently, <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sd}(X)([q])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sd</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sd}(X)([q])</annotation></semantics></math> may be explicitly described as the set of equivalence classes of conservative functors <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\colon(0&lt;\dots&lt;q)\to J_{X}^{\mathrm{op}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>X</mi><mi>op</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\colon(0&lt;\dots&lt;q)\to J_{X}^{\mathrm{op}}</annotation></semantics></math>, up to natural isomorphism of functors. In any case, let us emphasize that the subdivision of a symmetric <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex is an (augmented) ordinary <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex, not merely a symmetric one.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.13</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The geometric realizations of a symmetric <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex <math id="S3.Thmtheorem13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sd}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sd}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> are canonically homeomorphic.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since both geometric realization and barycentric subdivision preserve all small colimits, it suffices to construct a natural homeomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S3.SS4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.SS4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|I(-,[p])|\cong|\mathrm{sd}(I(-,[p]))|," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>sd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|I(-,[p])|\cong|\mathrm{sd}(I(-,[p]))|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">which is done in the usual way: the left hand side is <math id="S3.SS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, a non-empty subset <math id="S3.SS4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subset[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subset[p]</annotation></semantics></math> determines a face of <math id="S3.SS4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the corresponding vertex on the right hand side is sent to the barycenter of that face; extend to an affine map on each simplex.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.14</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Colimit presentations may be used to make many other definitions, or illuminate old ones. For example, the <em id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">join</em> <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\ast Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\ast Y</annotation></semantics></math> of two symmetric <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> may be defined by requiring <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I(-,[p]))\ast(I(-,[q]))=I(-,[p]\amalg[q])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∐</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I(-,[p]))\ast(I(-,[q]))=I(-,[p]\amalg[q])</annotation></semantics></math> and requiring <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\ast Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\ast Y</annotation></semantics></math> to preserve colimits in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> separately. The chains functor <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\mapsto C_{*}(X;R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\mapsto C_{*}(X;R)</annotation></semantics></math> that we defined above also preserves colimits, so it suffices to define it on representables. The shifted chains functor, sending <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{*}(X;R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{*}(X;R)</annotation></semantics></math> shifted so that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RX_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RX_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is in degree 0, is characterized up to natural isomorphism by its value on the point <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(-,[0])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(-,[0])</annotation></semantics></math> together with the properties that it sends join of symmetric <math id="S3.Thmtheorem14.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes to tensor product of chain complexes, and preserves all colimits.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.15</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Symmetric <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes may themselves be regarded as special cases of the <em id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">generalized cone complexes</em> of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACP15</a>, §2]</cite>. A cone is a topological space <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> together with an “integral structure,” i.e., a finitely generated subgroup of the group of continuous functions <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying a certain condition, and a generalized cone complex is glued out of cones along certain maps: by definition they are presented as colimits of cones, indexed by any small category.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The orthant <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{[p]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{[p]}</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with the group generated by the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+1</annotation></semantics></math> coordinate projections <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{[p]}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}\subset\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{[p]}\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}\subset\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is an example of a cone. The generalized cone complex corresponding to a symmetric <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is then the colimit of orthants, indexed by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem15.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="([p],x)\in J_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">([p],x)\in J_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5. </span>The tropical moduli space as a symmetric <math id="S3.SS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us return to the tropical moduli space <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we defined in
Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Graphs, tropical curves, and moduli ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. To illustrate how the definitions of this section
work for <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, we will give two descriptions that exhibit <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as
the geometric realization of a symmetric <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex. The
first description presents <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as a colimit of a diagram of
symmetric <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes; the second is an explicit description
as a functor <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\colon I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>op</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\colon I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The category <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> from §<a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2. The category 
          
            
              
              Γ
              g ‣ 2. Graphs, tropical curves, and moduli ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> has a unique final object: a
single vertex, of weight <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. For the first description of <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as a
colimit of a diagram of <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes, choose for each object
<math id="S3.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}\in\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}\in\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> a bijection <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\tau_{\bf G}\colon E({\bf G})\to[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>𝐆</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\tau_{\bf G}\colon E({\bf G})\to[p]</annotation></semantics></math> for
the appropriate <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0em">≥</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq-1</annotation></semantics></math>. This chosen bijection will be called
the <em id="S3.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">edge-labeling</em> of <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math>. The terminal object has <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=-1</annotation></semantics></math>,
and all non-terminal objects have <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. We require no
compatibility between the edge-labelings for different <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math>, but a
morphism <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\colon{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\colon{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> determines an injection</p>
<table id="S3.SS5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.SS5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p^{\prime}]\xrightarrow{\tau_{{\bf G}^{\prime}}^{-1}}E({\bf G}^{\prime})\xrightarrow{\phi^{-1}}E({\bf G})\xrightarrow{\tau_{\bf G}}[p]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">τ</mi><msup><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mover><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">ϕ</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></mrow></msup></mover><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">τ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐆</mi></msub></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p^{\prime}]\xrightarrow{\tau_{{\bf G}^{\prime}}^{-1}}E({\bf G}^{\prime})\xrightarrow{\phi^{-1}}E({\bf G})\xrightarrow{\tau_{\bf G}}[p],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the middle arrow is the induced bijection from the edges of
<math id="S3.SS5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> to the non-collapsed edges of <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> as in
Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 2.2. ‣ 2.2. The category 
          
            
              
              Γ
              g ‣ 2. Graphs, tropical curves, and moduli ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. This gives a functor
<math id="S3.SS5.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\colon\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}^{\mathrm{op}}\rightarrow I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>op</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\colon\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}^{\mathrm{op}}\rightarrow I</annotation></semantics></math> sending <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> to the
codomain <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>𝐆</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence induces a functor from
<math id="S3.SS5.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}^{\mathrm{op}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>op</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}^{\mathrm{op}}</annotation></semantics></math> to symmetric <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes, given as
<math id="S3.SS5.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}\mapsto I(-,F({\bf G}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}\mapsto I(-,F({\bf G}))</annotation></semantics></math>, whose colimit <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has geometric
realization <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X|=\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X|=\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and cone <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CX=M_{g}^{\mathrm{trop}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CX=M_{g}^{\mathrm{trop}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, colimit commutes with geometric realization by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem12" title="Lemma 3.12. ‣ 3.4. Colimit presentations and subdivision ‣ 3. Symmetric 
        
          Δ
        
      -complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.12</span></a>, so the geometric
realization of <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is the colimit of the functor
<math id="S3.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}\mapsto|I(-,F({\bf G}))|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}\mapsto|I(-,F({\bf G}))|</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}^{\mathrm{op}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>op</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}^{\mathrm{op}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖳𝗈𝗉</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Top}</annotation></semantics></math>. But
we have a homeomorphism <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|I(-,F({\bf G}))|=\Delta^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|I(-,F({\bf G}))|=\Delta^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S3.SS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]=F({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]=F({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, if <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}^{\prime}\in\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}^{\prime}\in\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is an object with
<math id="S3.SS5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime}+1</annotation></semantics></math> edges, then the injection of label sets <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\phi)\colon[p^{\prime}]\to[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\phi)\colon[p^{\prime}]\to[p]</annotation></semantics></math>
determined as above by a morphism <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\colon{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\colon{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, induces a
gluing of the simplex <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|I(-,F({\bf G}^{\prime}))|=\Delta^{p^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|I(-,F({\bf G}^{\prime}))|=\Delta^{p^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> to a face of
<math id="S3.SS5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|I(-,F({\bf G}))|=\Delta^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|I(-,F({\bf G}))|=\Delta^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. This agrees with the gluing obtained
from the gluing of <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma({\bf G}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma({\bf G}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> to a face of <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> in
Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 2.3. ‣ 2.3. Moduli space of tropical curves ‣ 2. Graphs, tropical curves, and moduli ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> by restricting to the length-one
subspaces <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{p}\subset\sigma({\bf G})=\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{E({\bf G})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{p}\subset\sigma({\bf G})=\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{E({\bf G})}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S3.SS5.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{p^{\prime}}\subset\sigma({\bf G})=\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{E({\bf G}^{\prime})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{p^{\prime}}\subset\sigma({\bf G})=\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{E({\bf G}^{\prime})}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the second description of <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as the geometric
realization of a symmetric <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex, we explicitly describe
a functor <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\colon I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>op</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\colon I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows. The
elements of <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> are equivalence classes of pairs <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bf G},\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bf G},\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}\in\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}\in\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\colon E({\bf G})\to[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\colon E({\bf G})\to[p]</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge labeling;
two edge-labelings are considered equivalent if they are related by an
isomorphism <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}\cong{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}\cong{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (including of course automorphisms).
Here <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> ranges over all objects in <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> with exactly <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+1</annotation></semantics></math>
edges. (Using that in §<a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2. The category 
          
            
              
              Γ
              g ‣ 2. Graphs, tropical curves, and moduli ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> we tacitly picked one
element in each isomorphism class in <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, the equivalence relation
is generated by actions of the groups <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Aut}({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Aut}({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math>.) This
defines <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\colon I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>op</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\colon I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}</annotation></semantics></math> on objects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Next, for each injective map <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota\colon[p^{\prime}]\to[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota\colon[p^{\prime}]\to[p]</annotation></semantics></math>, define the
following map <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(\iota)\colon X_{p}\to X_{p^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(\iota)\colon X_{p}\to X_{p^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>; given an element
of <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> represented by <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="({\bf G},\tau\colon E({\bf G})\to[p])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bf G},\tau\colon E({\bf G})\to[p])</annotation></semantics></math>,
contract the edges of <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> whose labels are not in
<math id="S3.SS5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota([p^{\prime}])\subset[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota([p^{\prime}])\subset[p]</annotation></semantics></math>, then relabel the remaining edges with labels
<math id="S3.SS5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> as prescribed by the map <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math>. The result is a
<math id="S3.SS5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math>-edge-labeling of some new object <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we set
<math id="S3.SS5.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(\iota)({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ι</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(\iota)({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> to be the element of <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{p^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{p^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Hereafter, we will use <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to refer to this symmetric <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex and write <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Delta_{g}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta_{g}|</annotation></semantics></math> for the topological space. To avoid double subscripts, we write <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}([p])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}([p])</annotation></semantics></math> for the set of <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-simplices of <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}(\Delta_{g})=\widetilde{H}_{k}(|\Delta_{g}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}(\Delta_{g})=\widetilde{H}_{k}(|\Delta_{g}|)</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="S3.SS5.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}([-1])=\{\ast\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}([-1])=\{\ast\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Graph complexes and cellular chains on <math id="S4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>.
From §<a href="#S3.SS5" title="3.5. The tropical moduli space as a symmetric 
          
            Δ
          
        -complex ‣ 3. Symmetric 
        
          Δ
        
      -complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> we read off the following presentation of the cellular chain complex <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(g)}=C_{*}(\Delta_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(g)}=C_{*}(\Delta_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. There is one generator <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\bf G},\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\bf G},\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> for each object <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}\in\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}\in\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and each bijection <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> to an object <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, subject to the relations <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\bf G},\omega]=\mathrm{sgn}(\sigma)[{\bf G}^{\prime},\omega^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\bf G},\omega]=\mathrm{sgn}(\sigma)[{\bf G}^{\prime},\omega^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> if there exists an isomorphism <math id="S4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> inducing the permutation <math id="S4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> of the set <math id="S4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]=\{0,\dots,p\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]=\{0,\dots,p\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The differential is</p>
<table id="S4.SS0.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial[{\bf G},\omega]=\sum_{i=0}^{p}(-1)^{i}[{\bf G}/e_{i},\omega|_{{\bf G}/e_{i}}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial[{\bf G},\omega]=\sum_{i=0}^{p}(-1)^{i}[{\bf G}/e_{i},\omega|_{{\bf G}/e_{i}}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(e_{i})=i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(e_{i})=i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega|_{{\bf G}/e_{i}}\colon E({\bf G}/e_{i})\to[p-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega|_{{\bf G}/e_{i}}\colon E({\bf G}/e_{i})\to[p-1]</annotation></semantics></math> makes the following diagram commute.</p>
<table id="S4.SS0.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS0.Ex2.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="65.41" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-45.76122874588133px" version="1.1" viewBox="31.82 45.76 137.19 65.41" width="137.19"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 31.815029137425192 65.40837122293138) translate(0,3.46) translate(31.82,0)"><g transform="translate(-31.82,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:4em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="55.33"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex2.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{E({\bf G}/e_{i})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{E({\bf G}/e_{i})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 31.81505025101183 -0.27673578005007266 L 31.81505025101183 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(24.42,0) translate(0,9.82) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-1.59)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.2em;--ltx-fo-height:0.53em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.35" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.26)" width="30.51"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex2.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\omega|_{{\bf G}\!/\!e_{i}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">ω</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐆</mi><mo lspace="0.142em" maxsize="0.710em" minsize="0.710em" rspace="0.142em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.710em">e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\omega|_{{\bf G}\!/\!e_{i}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(71.19,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 31.81505025101183 -0.27673578005007266 L 71.18509075396517 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 71.18509075396517 -0.27673578005007266 L 71.18509075396517 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -11.069600110696001 L 0.27673578005007266 -11.069600110696001" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(0,-23.62)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -23.622028524489327 L 0.27673578005007266 -11.069600110696001" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -23.622028524489327 L 0.27673578005007266 -23.622028524489327" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(71.19,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="24.64"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex2.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{[p\!-\!1]\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{[p\!-\!1]\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 87.38066854195854 -11.069600110696001 L 87.93414010205869 -11.069600110696001" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(87.66,0) translate(0,-17.35) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-4.39)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.68em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.79" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.79)" width="9.41"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex2.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\delta^{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\delta^{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(87.66,0) translate(0,-23.62)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 87.38066854195854 -23.622028524489327 L 87.93414010205869 -11.069600110696001" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 87.38066854195854 -23.622028524489327 L 87.93414010205869 -23.622028524489327" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(-21.29,0) translate(0,-34.69) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.48em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="34.29"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex2.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{E({\bf G})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{E({\bf G})\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 21.293579962996834 -34.9683644152354 L 21.293579962996834 -34.41489285513526" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(36.19,0) translate(0,-28.46) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-2.09)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.3em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="4.17" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 4.17)" width="6.97"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex2.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.700em">ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(76.18,0) translate(0,-34.69)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 21.293579962996834 -34.9683644152354 L 76.18181105387133 -34.41489285513526" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 76.18181105387133 -34.9683644152354 L 76.18181105387133 -34.41489285513526" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(76.18,0) translate(0,-34.69) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.06em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="14.65"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex2.m1.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{[p]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{[p]}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Proposition 3.8. ‣ 3.3. Cellular chains ‣ 3. Symmetric 
        
          Δ
        
      -complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a> specializes to the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is an isomorphism <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{H}_{k}(|\Delta_{g}|;\mathbb{Q})\cong H_{k}(C^{(g)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{H}_{k}(|\Delta_{g}|;\mathbb{Q})\cong H_{k}(C^{(g)})</annotation></semantics></math>. ∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(g)}\subset C^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(g)}\subset C^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> be the subcomplex spanned by those <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\bf G},\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\bf G},\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> which have a vertex with positive weight, and let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(g)}=C^{(g)}/B^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(g)}=C^{(g)}/B^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> be the quotient chain complex.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Hence <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by those <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\bf G},\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\bf G},\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> in which <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w(v)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w(v)=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all vertices <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. The boundary map on <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> is still an alternating sum of edge contractions, now identifying <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\bf G}/e,\omega|_{{\bf G}/e}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\bf G}/e,\omega|_{{\bf G}/e}]=0</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in E({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in E({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> is a loop. Comparing definitions, we get the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The chain complex <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to a shift of Kontsevich’s graph complex <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math>. In our grading conventions, the isomorphism is</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.SS0.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G^{(g)}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G^{(g)}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\to A^{(g)}_{k+2g-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\to A^{(g)}_{k+2g-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.SS0.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[{\bf G},\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[{\bf G},\omega]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mapsto[{\bf G},\omega].\qed" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" lspace="0.0835em" mathvariant="italic" separator="true">∎</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mapsto[{\bf G},\omega].\qed</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The next proposition implies that the homomorphism</p>
<table id="S4.SS0.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{H}_{k+2g-1}(|\Delta_{g}|;\mathbb{Q})\to H_{k}(G^{(g)})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{H}_{k+2g-1}(|\Delta_{g}|;\mathbb{Q})\to H_{k}(G^{(g)})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">induced by the quotient <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{(g)}\twoheadrightarrow A^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">↠</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{(g)}\twoheadrightarrow A^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism.
It is quite similar to the acyclicity result established in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">CGV05</a>, Theorem 2.2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The chain complex <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> has vanishing homology in all degrees.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The complex <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> calculates the reduced homology of the subspace of <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of graphs containing a vertex of positive weight. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">CGP19</a>]</cite> we shall show that this space is in fact contractible.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">For any <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in E({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in E({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math>, say <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is a </span><span id="S4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stem</span><span id="S4.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text"> if it separates a single weight-one vertex from the rest of <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., if <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> looks like</span></p>
<table id="S4.SS0.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\scriptstyle 1}\bullet\!\stackrel{{\scriptstyle e}}{{\text{---}}}\;?" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.052em">∙</mo><mover><mtext>—</mtext><mi>e</mi></mover><mi>?</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\scriptstyle 1}\bullet\!\stackrel{{\scriptstyle e}}{{\text{---}}}\;?</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">In particular a loop cannot be a stem. Notice that for distinct edges <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e,f\in E({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e,f\in E({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is not a loop, <math id="S4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is a stem in <math id="S4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if its image in <math id="S4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}/f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}/f</annotation></semantics></math> is a stem. Notice also that <math id="S4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> has a vertex of positive weight if and only if it admits a morphism from some <math id="S4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> having a stem.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> has a vertex of positive weight. Let <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> be the graph obtained from <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows: for any vertex <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> of weight <math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, other than a vertex of weight <math id="S4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> already separated from the rest of <math id="S4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> by a stem, replace <math id="S4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> with a single vertex of weight zero incident to <math id="S4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> stems. (If, and only if, <math id="S4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> is a single vertex of weight <math id="S4.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, it is necessary to suppress the resulting unstable vertex of weight <math id="S4.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and valence 2 in <math id="S4.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math>.) There is a canonical morphism <math id="S4.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\colon\widetilde{{\bf G}}\to{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐆</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\colon\widetilde{{\bf G}}\to{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> which contracts all stems. Moreover, <math id="S4.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{{\bf G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{{\bf G}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unique “maximal stem-uncontraction” of <math id="S4.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> in the following precise sense: for any morphism <math id="S4.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}\colon{\bf G}^{\prime}\to{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐆</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}\colon{\bf G}^{\prime}\to{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> that may be factored as a sequence of stem-contractions and isomorphisms, there exists a morphism <math id="S4.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\colon\widetilde{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\colon\widetilde{\bf G}\to{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi=\phi^{\prime}\circ\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi=\phi^{\prime}\circ\psi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(g),i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(g),i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the subcomplex of <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math> spanned by graphs <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\bf G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\bf G}}</annotation></semantics></math> with at most <math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> edges that are </span><span id="S4.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span><span id="S4.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text"> stems. Then the subcomplexes <math id="S4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(g),i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(g),i}</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0,\ldots,3g-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0,\ldots,3g-3</annotation></semantics></math>, filter <math id="S4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next, for each <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we claim vanishing of relative homology of the pair <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(B^{(g),i},B^{(g),i-1})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(B^{(g),i},B^{(g),i-1}).</annotation></semantics></math> The chain complex associated to this pair is generated by those <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\bf G},\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\bf G},\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> having a positive weight, satisfying in addition that <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> has exactly <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> non-stem edges. Furthermore, the boundary of <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\bf G},\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\bf G},\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> is a signed sum of 1-edge-contractions by stems.
We claim that this chain complex is a direct sum of subcomplexes <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(g),i}({\bf G})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(g),i}({\bf G}),</annotation></semantics></math> one for each <math id="S4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> non-stems that is maximal in the sense discussed above. Here, <math id="S4.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(g),i}({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(g),i}({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> is the subcomplex of <math id="S4.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(g),i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(g),i}</annotation></semantics></math> with a generator in degree <math id="S4.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S4.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\bf H},\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\bf H},\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bf H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bf H</annotation></semantics></math> isomorphic to a stem-contraction of <math id="S4.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\colon E({\bf H})\to[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\colon E({\bf H})\to[p]</annotation></semantics></math> a bijection, with relations <math id="S4.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[{\bf H},\omega]=\operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)[{\bf H}^{\prime},\omega^{\prime}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[{\bf H},\omega]=\operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)[{\bf H}^{\prime},\omega^{\prime}]</annotation></semantics></math> whenever there is a <math id="S4.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in S_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in S_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and an isomorphism <math id="S4.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf H}\to{\bf H}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐇</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf H}\to{\bf H}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> taking <math id="S4.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\circ\omega^{\prime}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\circ\omega^{\prime}.</annotation></semantics></math>
The fact that the chain complex does indeed split as a direct sum follows from the fact that if <math id="S4.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are both maximal stem-uncontractions of a given graph with a vertex of positive weight, then <math id="S4.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p8.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> admit morphisms to each other, hence are isomorphic in <math id="S4.p8.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{\Gamma}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now we claim that each <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(g),i}({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(g),i}({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic. Indeed, we will show that it is isomorphic to the rational cellular chain complex associated to the pair</span></p>
<table id="S4.SS0.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta^{|E({\bf G})|-1}/\!\Aut({\bf G}),Z/\!\Aut({\bf G}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.052em">/</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="0.052em">/</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta^{|E({\bf G})|-1}/\!\Aut({\bf G}),Z/\!\Aut({\bf G}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is the union of the <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> facets of <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{|E({\bf G})|-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{|E({\bf G})|-1}</annotation></semantics></math> that contain all vertices of <math id="S4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{|E({\bf G})|-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{|E({\bf G})|-1}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to stems of <math id="S4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;i&lt;|E({\bf G})|," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;i&lt;|E({\bf G})|,</annotation></semantics></math> there is a natural deformation retraction of <math id="S4.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{|E({\bf G})|-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{|E({\bf G})|-1}</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="S4.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>, and this retraction is <math id="S4.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant. Therefore the cellular chain complex of the pair must be acyclic.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">The cellular chain complex of the pair <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta^{|E({\bf G})|-1}/\!\Aut({\bf G}),Z/\!\Aut({\bf G}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.052em">/</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo rspace="0.052em">/</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta^{|E({\bf G})|-1}/\!\Aut({\bf G}),Z/\!\Aut({\bf G}))</annotation></semantics></math> has a generator in degree <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math>-equivalence class <math id="S4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[S,\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[S,\omega]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subseteq E({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subseteq E({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of stem edges and <math id="S4.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\colon E({\bf G})\setminus S\to[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\colon E({\bf G})\setminus S\to[p]</annotation></semantics></math> is a bijection, with relations
<math id="S4.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[S,\omega]=\operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)[S,\sigma\circ\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[S,\omega]=\operatorname{sgn}(\sigma)[S,\sigma\circ\omega]</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in S_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in S_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. There is a map from this chain complex to <math id="S4.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(g),i}({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(g),i}({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math>, sending <math id="S4.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[S,\omega]\mapsto[{\bf G}/S,\omega]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[S,\omega]\mapsto[{\bf G}/S,\omega]</annotation></semantics></math>, and this map is evidently surjective. In fact it is an isomorphism: if <math id="S4.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S,S^{\prime}\subseteq E({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S,S^{\prime}\subseteq E({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> are two sets of stem edges with <math id="S4.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}/S\cong{\bf G}/S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}/S\cong{\bf G}/S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, then by the maximality property of <math id="S4.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> discussed above, there is a morphism <math id="S4.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}\to{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐆</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}\to{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> making the diagram</span></p>
<table id="S4.SS0.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS0.Ex8.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="71.94" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-54.91831795183211px" version="1.1" viewBox="20.04 54.92 117.27 71.94" width="117.27"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 20.04435238245036 71.9378112311579) translate(0,3.46) translate(20.04,0)"><g transform="translate(-10.41,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.9em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.49" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.49)" width="12.51"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex8.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{{\bf G}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{{\bf G}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 10.406549035950256 -0.27673578005007266 L 10.406549035950256 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(29.84,0) translate(0,8.51) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-2.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.53em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.72" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.78)" width="7.26"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex8.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\cong}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.700em">≅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\cong}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(64.83,0)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 10.406549035950256 -0.27673578005007266 L 64.83199142133017 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 64.83199142133017 -0.27673578005007266 L 64.83199142133017 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -7.610350076103501 L 0.27673578005007266 -7.610350076103501" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(0,-32.78)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -32.779117730440106 L 0.27673578005007266 -7.610350076103501" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -32.779117730440106 L 0.27673578005007266 -32.779117730440106" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(64.83,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.9em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.49" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.49)" width="12.51"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex8.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{{\bf G}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{{\bf G}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 74.96178356364372 -7.610350076103501 L 75.51525512374386 -7.610350076103501" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(75.24,0) translate(0,-32.75)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 74.96178356364372 -32.75445706124818 L 75.51525512374386 -7.610350076103501" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 74.96178356364372 -32.75445706124818 L 75.51525512374386 -32.75445706124818" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(-20.04,0) translate(0,-43.85) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.3em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="31.79"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex8.m1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{{\bf G}/S\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{{\bf G}/S\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 20.04435238245036 -44.125453621186175 L 20.04435238245036 -43.57198206108603" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(29.84,0) translate(0,-35.34) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-2.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.53em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.14em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.72" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.78)" width="7.26"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex8.m1.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{\cong}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.700em">≅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{\cong}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(53.25,0) translate(0,-43.85)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 -6.92 2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -6.92 -2.77" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 20.04435238245036 -44.125453621186175 L 53.253152714538366 -43.57198206108603" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 53.253152714538366 -44.125453621186175 L 53.253152714538366 -43.57198206108603" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(53.25,0) translate(0,-43.85) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:2.58em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.86" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="35.67"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.SS0.Ex8.m1.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{{\bf G}/S^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{{\bf G}/S^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">commute. Moreover, any morphism <math id="S4.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}\to{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐆</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}\to{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math> is an automorphism. Therefore <math id="S4.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are in the same <math id="S4.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit.
This proves that <math id="S4.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(g),i}({\bf G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(g),i}({\bf G})</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic, as claimed.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to see that <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(g),0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(g),0}</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic. Generators are graphs in which every edge is a stem. It is not hard to classify such <math id="S4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf G}</annotation></semantics></math>: if <math id="S4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, there is one isomorphism class for each <math id="S4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\{0,\dots,g\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\{0,\dots,g\}</annotation></semantics></math>, given by a graph with a single central vertex of weight <math id="S4.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-h</annotation></semantics></math>, to which are attached <math id="S4.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> stems. (If <math id="S4.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>, then the classification is the same, but <math id="S4.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> must be in <math id="S4.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.) For <math id="S4.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, these graphs all admit odd automorphisms, so the corresponding generator for the graph complex vanishes. Thus <math id="S4.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{(g),0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{(g),0}</annotation></semantics></math> is rank two, generated by the <math id="S4.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=0</annotation></semantics></math> graphs, and the boundary of the <math id="S4.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=1</annotation></semantics></math> graph is the <math id="S4.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=0</annotation></semantics></math> graph.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Boundary complexes</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The theory of dual complexes for simple normal crossings divisors is
well-known. They may be constructed as <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes, with the <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex structure depending on a choice of total ordering on the irreducible components of the divisor. Many applications
involve the fact that the homotopy types (and even simple homotopy types) of <em id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">boundary complexes</em>,
the dual complexes of boundary divisors in simple normal crossings
compactifications, are independent of the choice of compactification. The same is also true for Deligne-Mumford (DM) stacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har17</a>]</cite>.
Boundary complexes were introduced and studied by Danilov in the 1970s
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dan75</a>]</cite>, and have become an important focus of research
activity in the past few years, with new connections to Berkovich
spaces, singularity theory, geometric representation theory, and the
minimal model program. See, for instance, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ste08</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">ABW13</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pay13</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">KX16</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sim16</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">dFKX17</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In order to apply combinatorial topological properties of <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to
study the moduli space of curves <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> using the
compactification by stable curves, we must account for the facts that
<math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> are stacks, not varieties, and that the
boundary divisor in <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> has normal crossings, but not simple
normal crossings. The latter of those two complications is the more
serious one; when the irreducible components of the strata have
self-intersections, the fundamental groups of strata may act
nontrivially by monodromy on the analytic branches of the boundary and
this needs to be accounted for. Once that is
properly understood, passing from varieties to stacks is relatively
straightforward.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we explain how dual complexes of normal crossings
divisors are naturally interpreted as symmetric <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes
and, in particular, the dual complex of the boundary divisor in the
stable curves compactification of <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally identified
with <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Dual complexes of simple normal crossings
divisors</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by recalling the notion of dual complexes of simple normal
crossings divisors, using the language of symmetric <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes introduced in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Symmetric 
        
          Δ
        
      -complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. In
§<a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. Dual complexes of normal crossings divisors ‣ 5. Boundary complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, we will explain how to interpret dual
complexes of normal crossings divisors in smooth DM
stacks as symmetric <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes of §<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3. Cellular chains ‣ 3. Symmetric 
        
          Δ
        
      -complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>. Here and throughout,
all of the varieties and stacks that we consider are over the complex
numbers, and all stacks are separated and DM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional smooth variety. Recall (cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sta17</a>, Tag 0BI9]</cite>) that a
<em id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">simple normal crossings</em> divisor is an effective Cartier divisor <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> which is Zariski locally cut out by <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1}\cdots x_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}\cdots x_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> for a regular system of parameters <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\dots,x_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\dots,x_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> in the local ring at any <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in D</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">strata</span> of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> may be defined inductively as
follows. The <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional strata of
<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> are the irreducible components of the smooth locus of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. For
each <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;d-1</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional strata are the irreducible
components of the regular locus of the complement of the union of all strata of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension greater than <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> has simple normal crossings, then the dual complex
<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(D)</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally understood as a regular symmetric
<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex whose geometric realization has one vertex for each
irreducible component of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, one edge for each irreducible component
of a pairwise intersection, and so on. The inclusions of faces
correspond to containments of strata. It is augmented, with
<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-simplices the set of irreducible components (equivalently,
connected components) of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently, using our
characterization of symmetric <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes in terms of
presheaves on the category <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> given in §<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Symmetric 
          
            Δ
          
        -complexes ‣ 3. Symmetric 
        
          Δ
        
      -complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>,
<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(D)</annotation></semantics></math> is the presheaf whose value on <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of pairs
<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y,\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y,\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> is a stratum of codimension <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+1</annotation></semantics></math>,
i.e., codimension <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is an ordering
of the components of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> that contain <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, with maps induced by
containments of strata. Dual complexes can also be defined in exactly
the same way for simple normal crossings divisors in DM stacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the literature, it is common to fix an ordering of the
irreducible components of the simple normal crossings divisor <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
The corresponding ordering of the vertices induces a
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex structure on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(D)</annotation></semantics></math>. Working with dual
complexes as symmetric <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes may be slightly more natural, in that
it avoids this choice of an ordering, and certainly it generalizes better to the
construction of dual complexes for divisors with (not necessarily simple) normal crossings as symmetric
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes, given in §<a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2. Dual complexes of normal crossings divisors ‣ 5. Boundary complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In the literature it is also commonly assumed that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is
irreducible, and hence there is no need for keeping track of
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-simplices and augmentations. This is sufficient for studying
one irreducible variety at a time, but comes with some
technical inconveniences. In particular, certain auxiliary
constructions, such as the étale covers and fiber products appearing later in this
section, do not preserve irreducibility. It is convenient to set up
the language in a way that applies without assuming
irreducibility.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Dual complexes of normal crossings divisors</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now discuss the generalization to normal crossings divisors <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> in
a smooth DM stack <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> which are not necessarily simple normal
crossings, i.e., the irreducible components of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> are not
necessarily smooth and may have self-intersections. This situation is
more subtle, even for varieties, due to monodromy. In the stack
case, when the boundary strata have stabilizers, the monodromy
may be nontrivial even for zero-dimensional strata. This phenomenon
appears already at the zero-dimensional strata of
<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> given by stable curves having nontrivial
automorphisms, i.e., the strata corresponding to (unweighted)
trivalent graphs of first Betti number <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> with nontrivial
automorphisms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth variety or DM stack, not necessarily irreducible.
Recall that a divisor <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> has normal crossings if and only
if there is an étale cover by a smooth variety <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\rightarrow X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\rightarrow X</annotation></semantics></math>
in which the preimage of <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is a divisor with simple normal
crossings.
In this situation, <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is a simple normal crossings divisor if all irreducible components are smooth.
Note that this étale local characterization of normal
crossings divisors is the same for varieties and DM stacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this situation the dual complex may be defined directly as a functor
<math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>op</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Sets</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Sets}</annotation></semantics></math>, in the following way. Let <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> denote the normalization of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset X</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]\in I</annotation></semantics></math> write</p>
<table id="S5.SS2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.SS2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{D}_{p}=(\widetilde{D}\times_{X}\dots\times_{X}\widetilde{D})\setminus\{(z_{0},\dots,z_{p})\mid\text{$z_{i}=z_{j}$ for some $i\neq j$}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mtext> for some </mtext><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}_{p}=(\widetilde{D}\times_{X}\dots\times_{X}\widetilde{D})\setminus\{(z_{0},\dots,z_{p})\mid\text{$z_{i}=z_{j}$ for some $i\neq j$}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">We have <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}_{0}=\widetilde{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}_{0}=\widetilde{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}_{-1}=X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}_{-1}=X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}_{p}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}_{p}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is a local complete intersection morphism whose conormal sheaf is a vector bundle of rank <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p+1)</annotation></semantics></math> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sta17</a>, Tag 0CBR]</cite>). In particular <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth over <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-p</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth over <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth variety or DM stack, let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> be a
normal crossings divisor, and write <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}_{p}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}_{p}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> for the
construction defined for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]\in I^{\mathrm{op}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>op</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]\in I^{\mathrm{op}}</annotation></semantics></math> above. In this situation, define
the symmetric <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(D)</annotation></semantics></math> by letting <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(D)_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(D)_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>
be the set of irreducible components (= connected components) of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We point out that in the case of stacks, the association <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]\mapsto\widetilde{D}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]\mapsto\widetilde{D}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> will only be a pseudofunctor, but the set of irreducible components will be functorial in <math id="S5.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]\in I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We note that a closed point of <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds precisely to a closed point <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> in a codimension <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> stratum of <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, together with an ordering <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p+1</annotation></semantics></math> local analytic branches of <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(D)_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(D)_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> may be described more transcendentally as the set of equivalence classes of pairs <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x^{\prime},\sigma^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x^{\prime},\sigma^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> if there is a path (continuous in the analytic topology) within the stratum connecting <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and that following the ordering of the branches along this path takes <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recall that a <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">regular</em> if the maps
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{p}\to|X|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{p}\to|X|</annotation></semantics></math> associated to <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in X_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in X_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> for all
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> are all injective. This definition makes sense equally
well for symmetric <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and is equivalent to the
condition that every edge of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has two distinct endpoints, i.e.,
for any <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}(e)\neq d_{1}(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}(e)\neq d_{1}(e)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The dual complex of a normal crossings divisor will be a regular symmetric
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex exactly when <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> has <em id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">simple</em> normal
crossings, meaning that every irreducible component of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is
smooth.
Indeed, the irreducible components of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> are smooth if and only if at every codimension <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> stratum of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, the two analytic branches belong to distinct irreducible components. This is equivalent to the condition that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}(e)\neq d_{1}(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}(e)\neq d_{1}(e)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in\Delta(D)_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in\Delta(D)_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The comparison of Definition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 5.2. ‣ 5.2. Dual complexes of normal crossings divisors ‣ 5. Boundary complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a> with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACP15</a>]</cite> uses the following étale descent result, whose proof we omit (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">CGP18</a>]</cite>). Let <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be normal crossings divisors in <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Suppose <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon X^{\prime}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon X^{\prime}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is étale and <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the preimage of <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> induces natural maps <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}^{\prime}_{p}\to\widetilde{D}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}^{\prime}_{p}\to\widetilde{D}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence a morphism <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(D^{\prime})\to\Delta(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(D^{\prime})\to\Delta(D)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S5.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,D)\mapsto\Delta(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,D)\mapsto\Delta(D)</annotation></semantics></math> is functorial for such étale maps. Together with the discussion in §<a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1. Dual complexes of simple normal crossings
divisors ‣ 5. Boundary complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>, the following étale descent property completely characterizes this functor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">With boundary complexes defined as in Definition <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Definition 5.2. ‣ 5.2. Dual complexes of normal crossings divisors ‣ 5. Boundary complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>,
the association <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,D)\mapsto\Delta(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,D)\mapsto\Delta(D)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies étale descent in
the sense that if <math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> is an étale cover and
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}=X_{0}\times_{X}X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}=X_{0}\times_{X}X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S5.SS2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.SS2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(D\times_{X}X_{1})\genfrac{.}{.}{0.0pt}{1}{\raise-2pt\hbox{$\scriptstyle\longrightarrow$}}{\raise 4pt\hbox{$\scriptstyle\longrightarrow$}}\Delta(D\times_{X}X_{0})\to\Delta(D)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac linethickness="0.0pt"><mpadded voffset="-2.0pt"><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">⟶</mo></mpadded><mpadded voffset="4.0pt"><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">⟶</mo></mpadded></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(D\times_{X}X_{1})\genfrac{.}{.}{0.0pt}{1}{\raise-2pt\hbox{$\scriptstyle\longrightarrow$}}{\raise 4pt\hbox{$\scriptstyle\longrightarrow$}}\Delta(D\times_{X}X_{0})\to\Delta(D)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is a coequalizer diagram.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 5.5</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the Whitney umbrella
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D=\{x^{2}y=z^{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\{x^{2}y=z^{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X=\mathbb{A}^{3}\setminus\{y=0\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\mathbb{A}^{3}\setminus\{y=0\},</annotation></semantics></math> as in
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACP15</a>, Example 6.1.7]</cite>. Calculating the dual complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(D)</annotation></semantics></math> by descent along the degree 2 étale cover given by a base change <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=u^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=u^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> gives the
half interval of Example <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Example 3.6. ‣ 3.2. Symmetric 
          
            Δ
          
        -complexes ‣ 3. Symmetric 
        
          Δ
        
      -complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>, presented as a quotient of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(-,[1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(-,[1])</annotation></semantics></math> by the action of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.6</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth variety or DM stack with the toroidal structure
induced by a normal crossings divisor <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\subset X</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the dual
complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(D)</annotation></semantics></math> is the symmetric <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex associated
to the smooth generalized cone complex <math id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma(X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> be an étale cover such that <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{0}=D\times_{X}X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{0}=D\times_{X}X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> has simple normal crossings in <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}=X_{0}\times_{X}X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}=X_{0}\times_{X}X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}=D\times_{X}X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>X</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}=D\times_{X}X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Endow <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with the toroidal structure induced by the simple normal crossings divisor <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma(X_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma(X_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the cone over <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(D_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(D_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACP15</a>, Proposition 6.1.2]</cite> describes <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma(X)</annotation></semantics></math> as the coequalizer in generalized cone complexes of <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma(X_{1})\genfrac{.}{.}{0.0pt}{1}{\raise-2pt\hbox{$\scriptstyle\longrightarrow$}}{\raise 4pt\hbox{$\scriptstyle\longrightarrow$}}\Sigma(X_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mfrac linethickness="0.0pt"><mpadded voffset="-2.0pt"><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">⟶</mo></mpadded><mpadded voffset="4.0pt"><mo mathsize="0.700em" stretchy="false">⟶</mo></mpadded></mfrac><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma(X_{1})\genfrac{.}{.}{0.0pt}{1}{\raise-2pt\hbox{$\scriptstyle\longrightarrow$}}{\raise 4pt\hbox{$\scriptstyle\longrightarrow$}}\Sigma(X_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. The result then follows from Lemma <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 5.4. ‣ 5.2. Dual complexes of normal crossings divisors ‣ 5. Boundary complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>, since the identification of symmetric <math id="S5.SS2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes with smooth
generalized cone complexes preserves all colimits.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Most important for our purposes is the special case where
<math id="S5.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Deligne–Mumford stable curves
compactification of <math id="S5.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is the boundary divisor. Modulo the translation from symmetric <math id="S5.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complexes to (smooth) generalized cone complexes given in Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem6" title="Corollary 5.6. ‣ 5.2. Dual complexes of normal crossings divisors ‣ 5. Boundary complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.6</span></a>, the following statement is one of the main results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">ACP15</a>]</cite> and we refer there for details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.7</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The dual complex of the boundary divisor in the moduli space of
stable curves with marked points
<math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Top weight cohomology</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth variety or DM stack of dimension <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math>. The rational singular cohomology of <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, like the rational cohomology of a smooth variety, carries a canonical mixed Hodge structure, in which the weights on <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are between <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\{2k,2d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\{2k,2d\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the graded pieces <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{j}^{W}H^{*}(\mathcal{X};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{j}^{W}H^{*}(\mathcal{X};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> vanish for <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&gt;2d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&gt;2d</annotation></semantics></math>, we refer to <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{2d}^{W}H^{*}(\mathcal{X};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{2d}^{W}H^{*}(\mathcal{X};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> as the <em id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">top weight cohomology</em> of <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.8</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be a smooth and separated DM stack of dimension <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> with a normal crossing compactification <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\overline{\mathcal{X}}\smallsetminus\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\overline{\mathcal{X}}\smallsetminus\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a natural isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S5.SS3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.SS3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{2d}^{W}H^{2d-k}(\mathcal{X};\mathbb{Q})\ \cong\ H_{k-1}(\Delta(\mathcal{D});\mathbb{Q})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{2d}^{W}H^{2d-k}(\mathcal{X};\mathbb{Q})\ \cong\ H_{k-1}(\Delta(\mathcal{D});\mathbb{Q}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">whose codomain is <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{H}_{k-1}(|\Delta(\mathcal{D})|;\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{H}_{k-1}(|\Delta(\mathcal{D})|;\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S5.Thmtheorem8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, we reduce to the case where <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> has simple normal crossings, by a finite sequence of blowups, as follows. Let <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}\rightarrow\overline{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the morphism obtained by first blowing up the zero-dimensional strata of <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, and then the strict transforms of the 1-dimensional strata, and so on. Then one readily checks that <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\prime}=\overline{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}\smallsetminus\overline{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\prime}=\overline{\mathcal{X}}^{\prime}\smallsetminus\overline{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> has simple normal crossings and <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\mathcal{D}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\mathcal{D}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is the barycentric subdivision of <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\mathcal{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\mathcal{D})</annotation></semantics></math>, as defined in §<a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Colimit presentations and subdivision ‣ 3. Symmetric 
        
          Δ
        
      -complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We may therefore assume that <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> has simple normal crossings. The remainder of the argument is essentially identical to
the proof for simple normal crossings divisors in algebraic varieties given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pay13</a>, Sections 2 and 4]</cite>. The one additional fact needed to go from varieties to DM stacks is that the cohomology of a smooth DM stack <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> with projective coarse moduli space <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is pure. To see this, note that the natural map <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{X}}\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{X}}\to X</annotation></semantics></math> induces an isomorphism <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(X;\mathbb{Q})\to H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{X}};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(X;\mathbb{Q})\to H^{*}(\overline{\mathcal{X}};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beh04</a>]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edi13</a>, Theorem 4.40]</cite>) and, since <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a compact Kähler <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>-manifold, its cohomology is pure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">PS08</a>, Theorem 2.43]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We briefly recall the argument: there is a complex of <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces</span></p>
<table id="S5.SS3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.SS3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="0\rightarrow\bigoplus_{i=1}^{r}H^{j}(\mathcal{D}_{i};\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{\delta_{0}}\bigoplus_{i_{0}&lt;i_{1}}H^{j}(\mathcal{D}_{i_{0}}\times_{\overline{\mathcal{X}}}\mathcal{D}_{i_{1}};\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{\delta_{1}}\bigoplus_{i_{0}&lt;i_{1}&lt;i_{2}}H^{j}(\mathcal{D}_{i_{0}}\times_{\overline{\mathcal{X}}}\mathcal{D}_{i_{1}}\times_{\overline{\mathcal{X}}}\mathcal{D}_{i_{2}};\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{\delta_{2}}\cdots," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>r</mi></munderover><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.111em">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">0</mn></msub></mover><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></munder><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.111em">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub></mover><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></munder><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0.1389em">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub></mover><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\rightarrow\bigoplus_{i=1}^{r}H^{j}(\mathcal{D}_{i};\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{\delta_{0}}\bigoplus_{i_{0}&lt;i_{1}}H^{j}(\mathcal{D}_{i_{0}}\times_{\overline{\mathcal{X}}}\mathcal{D}_{i_{1}};\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{\delta_{1}}\bigoplus_{i_{0}&lt;i_{1}&lt;i_{2}}H^{j}(\mathcal{D}_{i_{0}}\times_{\overline{\mathcal{X}}}\mathcal{D}_{i_{1}}\times_{\overline{\mathcal{X}}}\mathcal{D}_{i_{2}};\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{\delta_{2}}\cdots,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">whose cohomology gives the <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-graded pieces of the weight filtrations on the cohomology groups of <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">KK98</a>, Chapter 4, §2]</cite>. Set <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=0</annotation></semantics></math>; then the long exact sequence associated to the pair <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\overline{\mathcal{X}},\mathcal{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\overline{\mathcal{X}},\mathcal{D})</annotation></semantics></math>, together with the fact that <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{X}},\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k}(\overline{\mathcal{X}},\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is pure of weight <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, allows one to relate the cohomology of the above complex to <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr^{W}_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}(\mathcal{X};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr^{W}_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}(\mathcal{X};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is isomorphic by Poincaré duality to <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Gr^{W}_{2d}H^{2d-\bullet}(\mathcal{X};\mathbb{Q})^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em">∙</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr^{W}_{2d}H^{2d-\bullet}(\mathcal{X};\mathbb{Q})^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Applications</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now proceed to use the identification of top weight cohomology of <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> with reduced homology of the symmetric <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> developed in the preceding sections,
in combination with known nonvanishing and vanishing results for graph homology and cohomology of <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, to prove the applications stated in the introduction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem </span><a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is an isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S6.SS0.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.SS0.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{6g-6}^{W}H^{6g-6-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{\cong}\widetilde{H}_{k-1}(|\Delta_{g}|;\mathbb{Q})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">≅</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{6g-6}^{W}H^{6g-6-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})\xrightarrow{\cong}\widetilde{H}_{k-1}(|\Delta_{g}|;\mathbb{Q}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">identifying the reduced rational homology of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> with the top graded piece of the weight filtration on the cohomology of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\smallsetminus\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally identified with the dual complex <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\mathcal{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\mathcal{D})</annotation></semantics></math>, by Corollary <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem7" title="Corollary 5.7. ‣ 5.2. Dual complexes of normal crossings divisors ‣ 5. Boundary complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.7</span></a>. The theorem is therefore the special case of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 5.8. ‣ 5.3. Top weight cohomology ‣ 5. Boundary complexes ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.8</span></a> where <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{X}}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{X}}=\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We now prove our nonvanishing result for <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1a" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1a.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem </span><a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1a.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1a.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1a.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The cohomology <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1a.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero for <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1a.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1a.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=5</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1a.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 7</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1a.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> grows at least exponentially; precisely,</span></p>
<table id="S6.SS0.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.SS0.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})&gt;\beta^{g}+\text{constant}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">constant</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})&gt;\beta^{g}+\text{constant}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1a.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1a.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for any
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem1a.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&lt;\beta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&lt;\beta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1a.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0}\approx 1.3247\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>1.3247</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0}\approx 1.3247\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> is the real root of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1a.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{3}-t-1=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{3}-t-1=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>, we have a natural surjection <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g})\rightarrow H_{0}(G^{(g)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g})\rightarrow H_{0}(G^{(g)})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore the result follows from Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 2.7. ‣ 2.4. Kontsevich’s graph complex ‣ 2. Graphs, tropical curves, and moduli ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the nonvanishing unstable cohomology group <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gr_{12}^{W}H^{6}(\mathcal{M}_{3};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mn>12</mn><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gr_{12}^{W}H^{6}(\mathcal{M}_{3};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> found by Looijenga <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loo93</a>]</cite> is identified with the span of <math id="S6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[W_{3}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[W_{3}]</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}(G^{(3)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}(G^{(3)})</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, the nonvanishing, unstable, top weight cohomology that we describe, especially those corresponding to the spans of <math id="S6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[W_{g}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[W_{g}]</annotation></semantics></math> for odd <math id="S6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, may be seen as direct and natural generalizations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In comparison, the asymptotic size of the tautological ring of <math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded above by <math id="S6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\sqrt{g}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mi>g</mi></msqrt></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\sqrt{g}}</annotation></semantics></math> for a constant <math id="S6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, its Poincaré series is dominated coefficient-wise by that of the polynomial ring</p>
<table id="S6.SS0.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.SS0.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\ldots],\qquad\operatorname{deg}\kappa_{i}=2i." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\ldots],\qquad\operatorname{deg}\kappa_{i}=2i.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S6.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2i</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> has virtual cohomological dimension <math id="S6.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4g-5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4g-5</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har86</a>]</cite>. A rough bound may be obtained by calculating <math id="S6.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\ldots]_{2n}=p(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\mathbb{Q}[\kappa_{1},\kappa_{2},\ldots]_{2n}=p(n)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(n)</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of partitions of <math id="S6.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S6.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(p(n))\sim\pi\sqrt{2n/3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(p(n))\sim\pi\sqrt{2n/3}</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">HR17</a>, eq. (5.22)]</cite>, the dimension of the tautological ring is bounded by <math id="S6.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{n=1}^{2g-3}p(2n)&lt;2g\cdot p(2g)&lt;A\cdot B^{\sqrt{g}}+C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><msqrt><mi>g</mi></msqrt></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{n=1}^{2g-3}p(2n)&lt;2g\cdot p(2g)&lt;A\cdot B^{\sqrt{g}}+C</annotation></semantics></math> for constants <math id="S6.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, the Euler characteristic estimates by Harer–Zagier mentioned earlier imply that the size of the top weight part of <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> accounted for here is still negligible in comparison to the entire <math id="S6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{*}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> (and hence in comparison to the largest single Hodge number of <math id="S6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We also record the following nonvanishing result of odd-degree cohomology groups, as discussed in the introduction:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1b" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1b.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1b.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1b.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1b.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1b.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The cohomology groups <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1b.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{6};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{6};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1b.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{23}(\mathcal{M}_{8};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>23</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{23}(\mathcal{M}_{8};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1b.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{27}(\mathcal{M}_{10};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>27</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{27}(\mathcal{M}_{10};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> are nonzero.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>, the nontrivial classes in <math id="S6.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{3}(G^{(6)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{3}(G^{(6)})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{3}(G^{(8)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{3}(G^{(8)})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{7}(G^{(10)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{7}(G^{(10)})</annotation></semantics></math> discovered computationally in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">BNM</a>]</cite> implies nonvanishing of <math id="S6.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{6};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}(\mathcal{M}_{6};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{23}(\mathcal{M}_{8};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>23</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{23}(\mathcal{M}_{8};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{27}(\mathcal{M}_{10};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>27</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{27}(\mathcal{M}_{10};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The computations of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">BNM</a>]</cite> are extended, albeit by approximate (floating point) calculations, in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx39" title="" class="ltx_ref">KWŽ17</a>]</cite>, where it is also shown that <math id="S6.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{j\geq 0}H^{2j+1}(\mathsf{GC})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{j\geq 0}H^{2j+1}(\mathsf{GC})</annotation></semantics></math> is infinite dimensional.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Combining <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx39" title="" class="ltx_ref">KWŽ17</a>, Corollary 6]</cite> with Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>, <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>, and <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 2.7. ‣ 2.4. Kontsevich’s graph complex ‣ 2. Graphs, tropical curves, and moduli ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> yields the following dimension bound on top-weight odd-degree cohomology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</span></p>
<table id="S6.SS0.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.SS0.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}g\geq g^{\prime}\geq(2g+2)/3,\,\,i\geq 0\end{subarray}}\dim\Gr_{6g^{\prime}-6}^{W}H^{2i+1}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime}};\mathbb{Q})&gt;\beta^{g}+\text{constant}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.507em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Gr</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mi>g</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">constant</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}g\geq g^{\prime}\geq(2g+2)/3,\,\,i\geq 0\end{subarray}}\dim\Gr_{6g^{\prime}-6}^{W}H^{2i+1}(\mathcal{M}_{g^{\prime}};\mathbb{Q})&gt;\beta^{g}+\text{constant},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for any
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&lt;\beta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&lt;\beta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{0}\approx 1.3247\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>1.3247</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{0}\approx 1.3247\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> is the real root of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{3}-t-1=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{3}-t-1=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p12.1" class="ltx_p">We conclude with an application in the other direction, using known vanishing results for <math id="S6.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to reprove a recent vanishing result of Willwacher for graph homology.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem </span><a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_bold"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The graph homology groups <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}(G^{(g)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}(G^{(g)})</annotation></semantics></math> vanish for <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">The virtual cohomological dimension of <math id="S6.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4g-5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4g-5</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har86</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, <math id="S6.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-5}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-5}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">CFP12</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">MSS13</a>]</cite>. Therefore <math id="S6.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-6-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-6-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes for <math id="S6.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. The theorem follows, since <math id="S6.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-6-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-6-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> surjects onto <math id="S6.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}(G^{(g)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}(G^{(g)})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Hyperbolic surfaces</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here, we use the hyperbolic model for <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to construct the proper map <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\colon\mathcal{M}_{g}\to M_{g}^{\mathrm{trop}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\colon\mathcal{M}_{g}\to M_{g}^{\mathrm{trop}}</annotation></semantics></math> announced in (<a href="#S1.SS0.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.0.1</span></a>), and to give an explicit interpretation of the classes in <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{4g-6}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> arising from our main results. More details will appear in a sequel.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the hyperbolic interpretation of <math id="S7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, points are hyperbolic metrics on a closed genus <math id="S7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> surface <math id="S7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, up to isometric diffeomorphisms.
Let <math id="S7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\mathrm{thick}}\subset\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>thick</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\mathrm{thick}}\subset\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the subspace given in the hyperbolic model for <math id="S7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as those hyperbolic surfaces in which no non-trivial geodesic has length less than <math id="S7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, for a suitably small <math id="S7.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S7.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\mathrm{thick}}\subset\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>thick</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\mathrm{thick}}\subset\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a deformation retract <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">HZ86</a>, p. 476]</cite>. Equivalently, Harvey’s Borel–Serre type compactification of <math id="S7.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> or the Kato–Nakayama space associated to the boundary divisor in <math id="S7.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> may be used instead of <math id="S7.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\mathrm{thick}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>thick</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\mathrm{thick}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Its boundary consists of hyperbolic surfaces with at least one geodesic of length <math id="S7.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, but it is better regarded as an orbifold with corners: it is covered by orbifold charts of the form</p>
<table id="S7.SS0.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.SS0.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{S}\times\mathbb{R}^{T}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\mathrm{thick}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>thick</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{S}\times\mathbb{R}^{T}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\mathrm{thick}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p2.2" class="ltx_p">for finite sets <math id="S7.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> (varying from chart to chart).</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1. </span>A map of spaces</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> be a hyperbolic metric on a closed oriented 2-manifold <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be the dual graph of the nodal 2-manifold obtained from <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> by collapsing all closed geodesics of length smaller than some suitable <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, chosen once and for all. Each <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in E(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in E(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> then corresponds to a simple closed geodesic in <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Sigma,h)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Sigma,h)</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{e}&lt;\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{e}&lt;\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, and we let <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(e)=-\log(a_{e}/\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(e)=-\log(a_{e}/\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>. For sufficiently small <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, this recipe <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\Sigma,h]\mapsto(\Gamma,\ell)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\Sigma,h]\mapsto(\Gamma,\ell)</annotation></semantics></math> defines a (well defined) proper map</p>
<table id="S7.SS1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.SS1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\colon\mathcal{M}_{g}\to M_{g}^{\mathrm{trop}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>trop</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\colon\mathcal{M}_{g}\to M_{g}^{\mathrm{trop}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This is a model for the map (<a href="#S1.SS0.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.0.1</span></a>) in the introduction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2. </span>Generalizations of abelian cycles</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The injection <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}(G^{(g)})^{\vee}\to H_{4g-6-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}(G^{(g)})^{\vee}\to H_{4g-6-k}(\mathcal{M}_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> allows us to produce non-zero homology classes in the mapping class group from classes in <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{grt}_{1}\cong H^{0}(\mathsf{GC})\cong\prod_{g}H_{0}(G^{(g)})^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔤</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔱</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{grt}_{1}\cong H^{0}(\mathsf{GC})\cong\prod_{g}H_{0}(G^{(g)})^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is natural to ask for a more explicit description of the resulting homology classes. In this section we shall outline how to transport a class represented by a cocycle <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon G^{(g)}_{k}\to\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon G^{(g)}_{k}\to\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> through these isomorphisms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 7.1</span></span><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0em">≥</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq-1</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> be the space of isometry classes of pairs consisting of a hyperbolic genus <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> surface in <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\mathrm{thick}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>thick</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\mathrm{thick}}</annotation></semantics></math> together with an ordered <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-tuple of distinct geodesics of length <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, considered up to isometry of the surface preserving the ordered tuple of geodesics. Then <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{p+1}=I([p],[p])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{p+1}=I([p],[p])</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> by permuting the geodesics, and <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}\colon K_{p+1}\rightarrow K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}\colon K_{p+1}\rightarrow K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is induced by forgetting the <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\mbox{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\mbox{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> geodesic, defining a functor <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Spaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mi>op</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Spaces</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Spaces}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the coarse space of the orbifold <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\mathrm{thick}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>thick</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\mathrm{thick}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{p}\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\mathrm{thick}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>p</mi></msup><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>thick</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{p}\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\mathrm{thick}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the image of the map <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}\to K_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}\to K_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <math id="S7.Thmtheorem1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\emptyset\subset[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\emptyset\subset[p]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The symmetric <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-complex defined as <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]\mapsto\pi_{0}(K_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]\mapsto\pi_{0}(K_{p})</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. This may be seen by identifying the orbifold underlying <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> with an <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S^{1})^{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S^{1})^{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle over the complex analytic orbifold underlying <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}_{p}\smallsetminus d_{0}(\widetilde{D}_{p+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}_{p}\smallsetminus d_{0}(\widetilde{D}_{p+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, up to homotopy, or, more directly, by sending a hyperbolic surface with <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p+1)</annotation></semantics></math> ordered labeled geodesics to the dual graph of the nodal 2-manifold obtained by collapsing the geodesics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A cochain <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(g)}_{p}\to\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(g)}_{p}\to\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally identified (by extending to zero on graphs with non-zero weights) with a cochain <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in C^{p}(\Delta_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in C^{p}(\Delta_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition, such a cochain is a function <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\colon\Delta_{g}([p])=\pi_{0}(K_{p})\to\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\colon\Delta_{g}([p])=\pi_{0}(K_{p})\to\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> which is alternating under the action of <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence we may regard such a cochain as an element <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in H^{0}(K_{p};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in H^{0}(K_{p};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math>
on which a permutation <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in S_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in S_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math> acts as <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sgn}(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sgn}(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a cochain is a cocycle exactly when it is in the kernel of</p>
<table id="S7.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7.1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H^{0}(K_{p};\mathbb{Q})\otimes\mathbb{Q}^{\mathrm{sgn}})^{S_{p+1}}\xrightarrow{\sum(-1)^{i}(d_{i})^{*}}(H^{0}(K_{p+1};\mathbb{Q})\otimes\mathbb{Q}^{\mathrm{sgn}})^{S_{p+2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>sgn</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>sgn</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H^{0}(K_{p};\mathbb{Q})\otimes\mathbb{Q}^{\mathrm{sgn}})^{S_{p+1}}\xrightarrow{\sum(-1)^{i}(d_{i})^{*}}(H^{0}(K_{p+1};\mathbb{Q})\otimes\mathbb{Q}^{\mathrm{sgn}})^{S_{p+2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Next we wish to apply Poincare duality to <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a compact rational homology manifold with boundary. We first pin down orientations, i.e., fundamental classes <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[K_{p}]\in H_{d-p}(K_{p},\partial K_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[K_{p}]\in H_{d-p}(K_{p},\partial K_{p})</annotation></semantics></math>. The subset <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}(\partial K_{p+1})\subset\partial K_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">⊂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}(\partial K_{p+1})\subset\partial K_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and we have homomorphisms</p>
<table id="S7.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{d-p}(K_{p},\partial K_{p})\xrightarrow{\delta}H_{d-p-1}(\partial K_{p},d_{i}(\partial K_{p+1}))\xleftarrow{(d_{i})_{*}}H_{d-p-1}(K_{p+1},\partial K_{p+1})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝛿</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>←</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msub></mover><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{d-p}(K_{p},\partial K_{p})\xrightarrow{\delta}H_{d-p-1}(\partial K_{p},d_{i}(\partial K_{p+1}))\xleftarrow{(d_{i})_{*}}H_{d-p-1}(K_{p+1},\partial K_{p+1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where we write <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> for the connecting homomorphism of the triple. The map <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d_{i})_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d_{i})_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism by excision, and the orientations are chosen such that <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[K_{p+1}]=((d_{0})_{*})^{-1}\circ\delta([K_{p}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[K_{p+1}]=((d_{0})_{*})^{-1}\circ\delta([K_{p}])</annotation></semantics></math>, which forces <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((d_{i})_{*})^{-1}\circ\delta([K_{p}])=(-1)^{i}[K_{p+1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((d_{i})_{*})^{-1}\circ\delta([K_{p}])=(-1)^{i}[K_{p+1}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{*}([K_{p}])=\mathrm{sgn}(\sigma)[K_{p}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{*}([K_{p}])=\mathrm{sgn}(\sigma)[K_{p}]</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S7.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\in S_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\in S_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Poincaré duality, i.e., cap product with these fundamental classes, now identifies the homomorphism in (<a href="#S7.E1" title="In 7.2. Generalizations of abelian cycles ‣ 7. Hyperbolic surfaces ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>) with a homomorphism</p>
<table id="S7.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7.2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{d-p}(K_{p},\partial K_{p};\mathbb{Q})_{S_{p+1}}\xrightarrow{\sum((d_{i})_{*})^{-1}\circ\delta}H_{d-p-1}(K_{p+1},\partial K_{p+1};\mathbb{Q})_{S_{p+2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.710em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mover><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{d-p}(K_{p},\partial K_{p};\mathbb{Q})_{S_{p+1}}\xrightarrow{\sum((d_{i})_{*})^{-1}\circ\delta}H_{d-p-1}(K_{p+1},\partial K_{p+1};\mathbb{Q})_{S_{p+2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where the signs in both the <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{p+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{p+1}</annotation></semantics></math> action and the boundary homomorphism have canceled with those in the fundamental classes. A cocycle <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in C^{p}(\Delta_{g};\mathbb{Q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in C^{p}(\Delta_{g};\mathbb{Q})</annotation></semantics></math> gives a Poincaré dual <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{PD}([\alpha])\in H_{d-p}(K_{p},\partial K_{p};\mathbb{Q})_{S_{p+1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>PD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{PD}([\alpha])\in H_{d-p}(K_{p},\partial K_{p};\mathbb{Q})_{S_{p+1}}</annotation></semantics></math> in the kernel of (<a href="#S7.E2" title="In 7.2. Generalizations of abelian cycles ‣ 7. Hyperbolic surfaces ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>). Mapping into <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> sends all spaces into <math id="S7.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial^{p}\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\mathrm{thick}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>p</mi></msup><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>thick</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial^{p}\mathcal{M}_{g}^{\mathrm{thick}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the map (<a href="#S7.E2" title="In 7.2. Generalizations of abelian cycles ‣ 7. Hyperbolic surfaces ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
          M
          g" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>) fits into a commutative square</p>
<table id="S7.Ex1a" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S7.Ex1a.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="78.67" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-56.53259944533088px" version="1.1" viewBox="92.38 56.53 429.83 78.67" width="429.83"><g transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 92.37994358584777 78.67179966672288) translate(0,3.46) translate(92.38,0)"><g transform="translate(-71.04,0) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9.67em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.35em;font-size:10pt;" height="15.18" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="133.78"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S7.Ex1a.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textstyle{H_{d-p}(K_{p},\partial K_{p};\mathbb{Q})_{S_{p+1}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textstyle{H_{d-p}(K_{p},\partial K_{p};\mathbb{Q})_{S_{p+1}}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -12.414852283267175 L 0.27673578005007266 -12.414852283267175" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(0,-22.48) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-2.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.07em;--ltx-fo-height:0.53em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.18em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.69" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 7.26)" width="42.53"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S7.Ex1a.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{1/(p+1)!}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">!</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle{1/(p+1)!}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g transform="translate(0,-32.52)"><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 0 2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 0 0 A 13.84 13.84 45 0 1 -2.77 6.92" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path></g><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -32.523854467999776 L 0.27673578005007266 -12.414852283267175" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M -0.27673578005007266 -32.523854467999776 L 0.27673578005007266 -32.523854467999776" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><path style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;" d="M 71.03959702845017 -0.27673578005007266 L 71.03959702845017 0.27673578005007266" fill="none" stroke="#000000"></path><g transform="translate(92.59,0) translate(0,11.07) translate(4.15,0) translate(0,-3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.4em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="19.41"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S7.Ex1a.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle{(\ref{eq:16})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em"><a href="#S7.E2" title="In 7.2. Generalizations of abelian cycles ‣ 7. Hyperbolic surfaces ‣ Tropical curves, graph complexes, and top weight cohomology of 
      
        
          
